{"parentUuid":"843b2659-8872-45da-95c3-3d1ef9740e71","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgPHxZE4CNW44mFAPXFUS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3188,"cache_read_input_tokens":610177,"output_tokens":3148,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":552},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3188,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":3148,"cache_read_input_tokens":610177,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3188,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3188},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[{"type":"thinking_dropped","path":"messages.2.content.0","reason":"prefix_binding_mismatch"}],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":4871,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","serverClassifierRequest":"5db34f38-de2a-495d-b6be-bfdbbf160dda","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CgPHxXoEgaZ5Hs4oGo46h","requestRef":"05b7ddfc-dd6a-4acd-8784-fb503a5ad33e","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"bf1669a0-6042-439e-a5e5-363c1992f87e","timestamp":"2026-10-25T16:45:37.270Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","version":"2.1.308","gitBranch":"feat/addon-fill-after","slug":"purring-snuggling-matsumoto"}
{"parentUuid":"bf1669a0-6042-439e-a5e5-363c1992f87e","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgPHxZE4CNW44mFAPXFUS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"PR #197 is open with all 15 CI checks passing, but it did not deliver what the stopped fork's notes promised. I'm starting a fork on the actual cause.\n\n**What landed**\n- `fillAfter`: an option, off by default. `warmUp` now defaults to `false`.\n- README documentation for `lazy`, `fillAfter` and `warmUp`, which main lacked.\n- A new `fill.ts` (under the lint limit; `session.ts` is 299 of 300 lines), 59 addon tests, a changeset, and two new harness variants. It touches no crates.\n\n**Harness, Spectrum with 50 real stories** (answers identical to eager in every variant)\n\n| Variant | Total | First story | Median | Peak memory |\n| --- | --: | --: | --: | --: |\n| `main` as shipped | 1.0 s | 149 ms | 19 ms | 358 MB |\n| #197 default | 1.1 s | 146 ms | 19 ms | 324 MB |\n| `fillAfter: 16` | 1.0 s | 191 ms | 5 ms | 525 MB |\n| eager session | 0.3–0.4 s | 151 ms | 4 ms | 291 MB |\n\n**The premise didn't hold.**\n- `fillAfter` lowers the median story from 19 to 5 ms, but not the total, and it costs 170–420 MB of peak memory. On Chakra it takes peak memory from 422 to 857 MB for the same total.\n- That is why it ships off. You track memory as well as time.\n- The stopped fork's changeset claimed a project read end to end \"costs about what the old session did\". The measurements say it doesn't, and the fork that landed it didn't carry that claim over.\n\n**The weakness is still open.** Reading all stories on the lazy session takes about 1.0 s against 0.3–0.4 s eager. That matters for `storybook build`, which asks for every component. #197 found it is a tail of slow stories, not the median, and didn't go further.\n\n**My hypothesis, not verified.** In a lazy session, each new dependency name a component needs grows the dependency layer, and a layer growth replaces the resolution context. The context's memos (the shared Chakra- and Spectrum-style base types) are lost each time, so later components re-resolve them. The eager session loads every demand once, up front, so its memos persist. If that's right, the fix is to load the project's whole dependency demand set on the first request (loading is under 1% of busy CPU, 11–50 ms) and then resolve lazily against a stable context. That should bring the lazy total down toward eager without #197's memory cost, while keeping the first story fast.\n\nI'm starting a fork to confirm the cause with a profile and then fix it, capped at about 70 tool calls."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3188,"cache_read_input_tokens":610177,"output_tokens":3148,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":552},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3188,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":3148,"cache_read_input_tokens":610177,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3188,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3188},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[{"type":"thinking_dropped","path":"messages.2.content.0","reason":"prefix_binding_mismatch"}],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"5db34f38-de2a-495d-b6be-bfdbbf160dda","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgPHxXoEgaZ5Hs4oGo46h","requestRef":"05b7ddfc-dd6a-4acd-8784-fb503a5ad33e","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"1fa16452-8572-4a46-b0ac-0f33d8e3d5c4","timestamp":"2026-10-25T16:45:43.539Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","version":"2.1.308","gitBranch":"feat/addon-fill-after","slug":"purring-snuggling-matsumoto"}
{"parentUuid":"1fa16452-8572-4a46-b0ac-0f33d8e3d5c4","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgPHxZE4CNW44mFAPXFUS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017kRMS2CgijwwavLBKvsNt1","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Why a lazy session read end to end is 3x slower than eager","subagent_type":"fork","isolation":"worktree","name":"lazy-tail","prompt":"Track Z: close the lazy-session end-to-end gap. Branch off origin/main; open your PR with base `main` (#196 LSP removal and #197 addon fillAfter are open and independent; if you need the harness variants from #197 (`caliper-fill`, `caliper-shipped`) merge origin/feat/addon-fill-after into a scratch copy only, do not depend on it). Problem: reading a whole project through a lazy session is ~3x slower than the eager session: Spectrum 50 real stories 1.0-1.1 s lazy vs 0.3-0.4 s eager (median story 19 ms vs 4 ms; first story equal ~150 ms); Chakra similar. #197's `fillAfter` (resolve the rest in one background pass after 16 components) cut the median to 5 ms but not the total and cost +170-420 MB, so it ships off. Hypothesis (UNVERIFIED, verify first): in a lazy session each newly demanded dependency name grows the dependency layer (`Revision::grow` in crates/core/src/pipeline/revision/lazy.rs and dependency_types::load extending a layer from `previous`), and a layer growth replaces the `Engine`/resolution context, throwing away its memos (interface chains, named results, member reads: see `Memo`, `props_reads`, `Engine` in lazy.rs; the doc says memos carry over a growth only where safe), so later components re-resolve shared bases (Spectrum/Chakra style props) over and over; the eager session loads all demands once so its memos persist. Do: (1) MEASURE with lazy_bench (crates/core/examples/lazy_bench.rs, add counters temporarily or use `sessionStats`/`lazy_stats`): number of layer growths, memo hit/miss and resolve time per request over a 50-component sequence on Spectrum and Chakra with loadDependencies; time spent in grow/load vs resolve; confirm or refute the hypothesis with numbers; (2) if confirmed, fix at the root: e.g. on the first request (or at session start in the background) compute the project's whole dependency demand set from the parse-level index (the same demands the eager session loads; it is cheap: dependency loading is <1% of busy CPU, 11-50 ms) and load that layer once, so later requests never grow it and the context/memos persist; OR make growth keep valid memos (preserve entries whose reads do not touch the grown names; Memo recordings carry reads, so a recording is valid if none of its reads' keys changed). Prefer the simplest sound option; keep the first-story latency low (~150 ms Spectrum, 55-124 ms Chakra) and peak RSS no worse than today's lazy (Spectrum ~324-358 MB, Chakra ~422 MB); (3) results must be identical to batch: `lazy_parity` (fixtures, and `--lazy all` in `just equivalence`/crates/core/tests/lazy_parity.rs over corpora) and the Storybook harness answers identical; (4) re-run the harness end to end: node packages/storybook/scripts/storybook-parity.mjs --corpora /Users/gabe/.claude/jobs/ba6a68d6/tmp/bench-corpus --only spectrum,chakra --limit 50 --out <file> after `pnpm --filter caliper-docgen-napi build:napi` and `pnpm --filter caliper-docgen-storybook build` and report first story, median, total, peak RSS vs main. If the hypothesis is wrong, find the real cause with a profile (macOS `sample`) and report it even if you cannot fix it. Avoid the files PR #177-#192 changed unless needed (they are merged now; just rebase). Gates: exact-value tests (a test that a sequence of requests triggers at most one layer growth, or that memos survive growth), lazy parity, `just lint doc-check machete typos` and `cargo nextest run --workspace --exclude caliper-docgen-napi --locked` exit codes read directly (never pipe through tail), CI green, changeset, update docs/architecture/pipeline-orchestration.md and docs/adr/0016 if the contract changes. Time-box ~70 tool calls. Commits end with Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/REDACTED; PR body ends with https://claude.ai/code/REDACTED; never push main/force-push/merge; /usr/bin/git with sandbox off, one plain git command per call."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3188,"cache_read_input_tokens":610177,"output_tokens":3148,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":552},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3188,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":3148,"cache_read_input_tokens":610177,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3188,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3188},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[{"type":"thinking_dropped","path":"messages.2.content.0","reason":"prefix_binding_mismatch"}],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_017kRMS2CgijwwavLBKvsNt1":{"description":"Why a lazy session read end to end is 3x slower than eager","subagent_type":"fork","isolation":"worktree","name":"lazy-tail","prompt":"Track Z: close the lazy-session end-to-end gap. Branch off origin/main; open your PR with base `main` (#196 LSP removal and #197 addon fillAfter are open and independent; if you need the harness variants from #197 (`caliper-fill`, `caliper-shipped`) merge origin/feat/addon-fill-after into a scratch copy only, do not depend on it). Problem: reading a whole project through a lazy session is ~3x slower than the eager session: Spectrum 50 real stories 1.0-1.1 s lazy vs 0.3-0.4 s eager (median story 19 ms vs 4 ms; first story equal ~150 ms); Chakra similar. #197's `fillAfter` (resolve the rest 