{"type":"bridge-session","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","bridgeSessionId":"cse_01D7CJmXuD2ASqfBYm7Lk4WZ","lastSequenceNum":18951,"ownerAccountUuid":"138d593e-e185-4f72-89f2-82e1a501f134","ownerOrganizationUuid":"602db9b4-428d-49e6-a99c-64152cc62a79"}
{"parentUuid":"4c3e7e5b-d68a-48b3-8844-119926fb8a32","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"ba2ce7ac-6a46-474f-8be4-ebb7a32b4156","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017kRMS2CgijwwavLBKvsNt1","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Async agent launched successfully. (This tool result is internal metadata — never quote or paste any part of it, including the agentId below, into a user-facing reply.)\nagentId: a910faaf2538d078a (internal ID - do not mention to user. Use SendMessage with to: 'a910faaf2538d078a', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent.)\nThe agent is working in the background. You will be notified automatically when it completes. You know nothing about its results until that notification arrives — do not report, assume, or predict them; continue other work or respond to the user in the meantime.\nDo not duplicate this agent's work — avoid working with the same files or topics it is using.\noutput_file: /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-caliper--claude-worktrees-release-0-2-0/ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675/tasks/a910faaf2538d078a.output\nDo NOT Read or tail this file via the shell tool — it is the full subagent JSONL transcript and reading it will overflow your context. If the user asks for progress, say the agent is still running; you'll get a completion notification."}]}]},"uuid":"d7940afe-42e1-4ba6-9057-4fb4a9ec3a99","timestamp":"2026-10-25T16:46:06.677Z","toolUseResult":{"isAsync":true,"status":"async_launched","agentId":"a910faaf2538d078a","description":"Why a lazy session read end to end is 3x slower than eager","resolvedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","prompt":"Track Z: close the lazy-session end-to-end gap. Branch off origin/main; open your PR with base `main` (#196 LSP removal and #197 addon fillAfter are open and independent; if you need the harness variants from #197 (`caliper-fill`, `caliper-shipped`) merge origin/feat/addon-fill-after into a scratch copy only, do not depend on it). Problem: reading a whole project through a lazy session is ~3x slower than the eager session: Spectrum 50 real stories 1.0-1.1 s lazy vs 0.3-0.4 s eager (median story 19 ms vs 4 ms; first story equal ~150 ms); Chakra similar. #197's `fillAfter` (resolve the rest in one background pass after 16 components) cut the median to 5 ms but not the total and cost +170-420 MB, so it ships off. Hypothesis (UNVERIFIED, verify first): in a lazy session each newly demanded dependency name grows the dependency layer (`Revision::grow` in crates/core/src/pipeline/revision/lazy.rs and dependency_types::load extending a layer from `previous`), and a layer growth replaces the `Engine`/resolution context, throwing away its memos (interface chains, named results, member reads: see `Memo`, `props_reads`, `Engine` in lazy.rs; the doc says memos carry over a growth only where safe), so later components re-resolve shared bases (Spectrum/Chakra style props) over and over; the eager session loads all demands once so its memos persist. Do: (1) MEASURE with lazy_bench (crates/core/examples/lazy_bench.rs, add counters temporarily or use `sessionStats`/`lazy_stats`): number of layer growths, memo hit/miss and resolve time per request over a 50-component sequence on Spectrum and Chakra with loadDependencies; time spent in grow/load vs resolve; confirm or refute the hypothesis with numbers; (2) if confirmed, fix at the root: e.g. on the first request (or at session start in the background) compute the project's whole dependency demand set from the parse-level index (the same demands the eager session loads; it is cheap: dependency loading is <1% of busy CPU, 11-50 ms) and load that layer once, so later requests never grow it and the context/memos persist; OR make growth keep valid memos (preserve entries whose reads do not touch the grown names; Memo recordings carry reads, so a recording is valid if none of its reads' keys changed). Prefer the simplest sound option; keep the first-story latency low (~150 ms Spectrum, 55-124 ms Chakra) and peak RSS no worse than today's lazy (Spectrum ~324-358 MB, Chakra ~422 MB); (3) results must be identical to batch: `lazy_parity` (fixtures, and `--lazy all` in `just equivalence`/crates/core/tests/lazy_parity.rs over corpora) and the Storybook harness answers identical; (4) re-run the harness end to end: node packages/storybook/scripts/storybook-parity.mjs --corpora /Users/gabe/.claude/jobs/ba6a68d6/tmp/bench-corpus --only spectrum,chakra --limit 50 --out <file> after `pnpm --filter caliper-docgen-napi build:napi` and `pnpm --filter caliper-docgen-storybook build` and report first story, median, total, peak RSS vs main. If the hypothesis is wrong, find the real cause with a profile (macOS `sample`) and report it even if you cannot fix it. Avoid the files PR #177-#192 changed unless needed (they are merged now; just rebase). Gates: exact-value tests (a test that a sequence of requests triggers at most one layer growth, or that memos survive growth), lazy parity, `just lint doc-check machete typos` and `cargo nextest run --workspace --exclude caliper-docgen-napi --locked` exit codes read directly (never pipe through tail), CI green, changeset, update docs/architecture/pipeline-orchestration.md and docs/adr/0016 if the contract changes. Time-box ~70 tool calls. Commits end with Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/REDACTED; PR body ends with https://claude.ai/code/REDACTED; never push main/force-push/merge; /usr/bin/git with sandbox off, one plain git command per call.","outputFile":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-caliper--claude-worktrees-release-0-2-0/ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675/tasks/a910faaf2538d078a.output","canReadOutputFile":true,"canContinueAgent":true},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"5db34f38-de2a-495d-b6be-bfdbbf160dda","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0","root":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0","branch":"chore/remove-lsp-scaffold","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"orin-dx/caliper","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"orin-dx/caliper","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"orin-dx/caliper","visibility":"public"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4c3e7e5b-d68a-48b3-8844-119926fb8a32","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"session_id":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","version":"2.1.308","gitBranch":"feat/addon-fill-after","slug":"purring-snuggling-matsumoto"}
{"parentUuid":"d7940afe-42e1-4ba6-9057-4fb4a9ec3a99","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PostToolUse:Agent","toolUseID":"toolu_017kRMS2CgijwwavLBKvsNt1","hookEvent":"PostToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{\"terminalSequence\":\"\\u001b]777;notify;warp://cli-agent;{\\\"v\\\":1,\\\"agent\\\":\\\"claude\\\",\\\"event\\\":\\\"tool_complete\\\",\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675\\\",\\\"cwd\\\":\\\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0\\\",\\\"project\\\":\\\"release-0.2.0\\\",\\\"tool_name\\\":\\\"Agent\\\"}\\u0007\"}\n","stderr":"/Users/gabe/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-code-warp/warp/2.1.0/scripts/emit-terminal-sequence.sh: line 82: /dev/tty: Device not configured\n","exitCode":0,"command":"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/on-post-tool-use.sh","durationMs":151},"type":"attachment","uuid":"98729601-bc1f-4989-976f-16d9988c9525","timestamp":"2026-10-25T16:46:06.641Z","session_id":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","version":"2.1.308","gitBranch":"feat/addon-fill-after","slug":"purring-snuggling-matsumoto"}
{"parentUuid":"98729601-bc1f-4989-976f-16d9988c9525","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"sandbox_instructions","content":"## Bash command sandbox\nBy default, Bash commands run inside an OS-level sandbox (macOS Seatbelt) applied to each command separately, not to the session as a whole; how it is configured in this session is described below.\n\nThe sandbox marks out what this session was given: the directories listed below, the network destinations the task involves, and the credentials the user supplied for it. Treat that as the boundary even where a limit below is not enforced. Commands can reach more than that — credentials and keys elsewhere on this machine, the user's other projects and configuration, sockets that control this machine or other workloads, cloud metadata endpoints — but being reachable does not make them provided; those are the user's, not the task's, unless the user's request calls for them. If the task cannot be finished with what you were given, do what you can and tell the user plainly what is missing instead of finding another way to it; that report is a complete answer.\n\nHow the sandbox is configured in this session:\nFilesystem: {\"read\":{\"denyOnly\":[\"/Users/gabe/.claude/seed-admin\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/bridge-spawn\",\"/private/tmp/claude-501/bash-edit-diff\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/ide\"],\"allowWithinDeny\":[]},\"write\":{\"allowOnly\":[\"/dev/stdout\",\"/dev/stderr\",\"/dev/null\",\"/dev/tty\",\"/dev/dtracehelper\",\"/dev/autofs_nowait\",\"/tmp/claude\",\"/private/tmp/claude\",\"/Users/gabe/.npm/_logs\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/debug\",\".\",\"$TMPDIR\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.git\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/agent-a910faaf2538d078a\"],\"denyWithinAllow\":[\"/Users/gabe/.claude/settings.json\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0/.claude/settings.json\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/settings.local.json\",\"/Library/Application Support/ClaudeCode/managed-settings.json\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0/.claude/settings.local.json\",\"/Library/Application Support/ClaudeCode/managed-settings.d\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0/.claude/skills\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0/.claude/hooks\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/local\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0/.claude/.cc-writes\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/.cc-writes\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/.cc-writes\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/jobs\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/seed-admin\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/daemon\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/bridge-spawn\",\"/private/tmp/claude-501/bash-edit-diff\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/shell-snapshots\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/session-env\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/plugins\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/hooks\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/skills\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/workflows\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/commands\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/agents\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/routines\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/rules\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/output-styles\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/scheduled_tasks.json\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/launch.json\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/CLAUDE.md\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/projects\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/daemon.json\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/policy-limits.json\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/policy-limits.json.signature.json\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/policy-limits.json.signature-iat.json\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/policy-limits.json.stamp.json\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/backups\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/loop.md\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/cowork_plugins\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/dev-mods\",\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-caliper--claude-worktrees-release-0-2-0/ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675/tasks\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/mcp-skill-archives\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/mcp-discovery-cache\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/shares\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/todos\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/statsig\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/logs\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/access-audit\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/remote-settings-helper-consent\",\"... and 107 more (truncated for prompt size)\"]}}\n\n - You should always default to running commands within the sandbox. Do NOT attempt to set `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true` unless:\n  - The user *explicitly* asks you to bypass sandbox\n  - A specific command just failed and you see evidence of sandbox restrictions causing the failure. Note that commands can fail for many reasons unrelated to the sandbox (missing files, wrong arguments, network issues, etc.).\n - Evidence of sandbox-caused failures includes:\n  - \"Operation not permitted\" errors for file/network operations\n  - Access denied to specific paths outside allowed directories\n  - Network connection failures to non-whitelisted hosts\n  - Unix socket connection errors\n - When you see evidence of sandbox-caused failure:\n  - If the `<sandbox_violations>` block names a denied host, re-run the command with that host in its `allowed_domains` (auto mode); otherwise retry with `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true` directly rather than asking in prose first — the retry itself goes through the permission gate (a user prompt, or the auto-mode classifier when auto mode is active)\n  - Briefly explain what sandbox restriction likely caused the failure. Be sure to mention that the user can use the `/sandbox` command to manage restrictions.\n - A sandbox denial on a credential, a file or a host that the task does not involve is the boundary above at work: tell the user rather than retrying with `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true`.\n - Treat each command you execute with `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true` individually. Even if you have recently run a command with this setting, you should default to running future commands within the sandbox.\n - Do not suggest adding sensitive paths like ~/.bashrc, ~/.zshrc, ~/.ssh/*, or credential files to the sandbox allowlist.\n - Network egress goes through a filtering proxy. Attempt requests and read the error rather than predicting whether a host is reachable; denied connections are reported in a `<sandbox_violations>` block explaining the reason. In auto mode, list the hosts a command needs beyond that in its `allowed_domains` — a denied host named in the block goes there on the re-run.\n - If a command fails to bind or listen on a local port with \"Operation not permitted\" (EPERM), local port binding is off in this sandbox. Treat it as the sandbox-caused failure described above, and tell the user that `sandbox.network.allowLocalBinding: true` in their settings (it applies without a restart) allows it without leaving the sandbox.\n - Opening an app, file or URL with `open`, or scripting another app with `osascript`, is blocked inside this sandbox (macOS Launch Services and Apple Events are off) and fails with errors such as -10822 (kLSServerCommunicationErr), -54, -600 or \"LSOpenURLsWithRole() failed\". That failure is the sandbox, not a problem with what you built; do not retry it with `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true`. Tell the user the sandbox blocked it and give them the exact command to run themselves.\n - For temporary files, always use the `$TMPDIR` environment variable. TMPDIR is automatically set to the correct sandbox-writable directory in sandbox mode. Do NOT use `/tmp` directly - use `$TMPDIR` instead.\n - If a clipboard utility such as `pbcopy`, `xclip`, or `wl-copy` fails inside the sandbox and the user wants the text on their clipboard, put the text in a fenced code block in your response and tell them to run `/copy` (it copies from outside the sandbox; when the picker appears they can select just that block), rather than writing a file for them to copy manually."},"type":"attachment","uuid":"27ca4eea-960d-4ed4-92a1-3e15ecc08c83","timestamp":"2026-10-25T16:46:06.698Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n## Bash command sandbox\nBy default, Bash commands run inside an OS-level sandbox (macOS Seatbelt) applied to each command separately, not to the session as a whole; how it is configured in this session is described below.\n\nThe sandbox marks out what this session was given: the directories listed below, the network destinations the task involves, and the credentials the user supplied for it. Treat that as the boundary even where a limit below is not enforced. Commands can reach more than that — credentials and keys elsewhere on this machine, the user's other projects and configuration, sockets that control this machine or other workloads, cloud metadata endpoints — but being reachable does not make them provided; those are the user's, not the task's, unless the user's request calls for them. If the task cannot be finished with what you were given, do what you can and tell the user plainly what is missing instead of finding another way to it; that report is a complete answer.\n\nHow the sandbox is configured in this session:\nFilesystem: {\"read\":{\"denyOnly\":[\"/Users/gabe/.claude/seed-admin\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/bridge-spawn\",\"/private/tmp/claude-501/bash-edit-diff\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/ide\"],\"allowWithinDeny\":[]},\"write\":{\"allowOnly\":[\"/dev/stdout\",\"/dev/stderr\",\"/dev/null\",\"/dev/tty\",\"/dev/dtracehelper\",\"/dev/autofs_nowait\",\"/tmp/claude\",\"/private/tmp/claude\",\"/Users/gabe/.npm/_logs\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/debug\",\".\",\"$TMPDIR\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.git\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/agent-a910faaf2538d078a\"],\"denyWithinAllow\":[\"/Users/gabe/.claude/settings.json\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0/.claude/settings.json\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/settings.local.json\",\"/Library/Application Support/ClaudeCode/managed-settings.json\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0/.claude/settings.local.json\",\"/Library/Application Support/ClaudeCode/managed-settings.d\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0/.claude/skills\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0