{"parentUuid":"aecdeaa3-08f6-48e0-8694-4235876f8e46","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgPSWH342LTdrU9dyoozZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"These are rough estimates with low confidence. My only measured data point against react-docgen is the site's shared Node harness, and it is a poor basis because Node startup dominates it.\n\n**Speed**\n- **Measured, site's harness (21 libraries, includes Node startup):** caliper 2.96 s, react-docgen 3.39 s, RDT 10.37 s. That is only 1.15× faster than react-docgen.\n- **Compute only, my estimate.** The page says the harness time is mostly Node startup that all three tools pay (caliper's binary alone is about 50 ms, so startup is about 2.9 s). That leaves react-docgen at about 0.5 s of real work and RDT at about 7.5 s.\n  - Caliper is then roughly 10× faster than react-docgen and roughly 150× faster than RDT on those libraries.\n  - On the five large corpora I'd say **about 5–15× faster than react-docgen**, with a median near 8×. That is much lower than the roughly 30× against RDT, because react-docgen only parses syntax and never builds a type program.\n- **Inside Storybook,** Node is already running, so the compute comparison is the relevant one. The addon's own per-story cost is a few milliseconds (Spectrum median 4–5 ms), against react-docgen's per-file transform cost, which I haven't measured.\n\n**Memory**\n- react-docgen holds no type program, so it should be light. I'd estimate caliper at **about the same, or 1–2× less**, and on a large project like Chakra with dependencies possibly slightly more (about 345 MB batch). Honest answer: I don't know, and this is the weakest of the three estimates.\n\n**Quality**\n- This is where caliper should win, not on speed. react-docgen is syntactic: it reads the props type as written and follows imports within the project, but it does not read installed packages' declarations or evaluate utility, conditional and mapped types.\n- Caliper resolves inherited and library props (Chakra, Radix and Ark components, `Pick<ComponentProps<…>>`, union-typed props) and produces enum and union controls Storybook can render. The site's own table says react-docgen and RDT cannot read `.d.ts`.\n- The addon already works this way: it answers a component itself, and hands only the degraded 0–10% to the next provider, which would be react-docgen.\n\n**Plain version:** modestly faster than Storybook's default (5–15× on compute, 1.15× if you count Node startup), similar memory, and the real difference is that it reports props react-docgen can't see. I'd lead with accuracy and not speed when comparing against react-docgen, because the speed multiple is small and the evidence is thin."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1268,"cache_read_input_tokens":672688,"output_tokens":1829,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":949},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1268,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1829,"cache_read_input_tokens":672688,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1268,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1268},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"c2b42aeb-38e8-441e-9043-f1a108d1b509","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgPSWGejr68UR7QgrcZH4","requestRef":"86d762a2-adb4-43df-a3e3-370e2454478f","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"7c414263-88f1-4cae-a40f-c588a6992a51","timestamp":"2026-10-25T18:37:45.251Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675"