{"parentUuid":"9757204e-a9b1-485e-bd26-b2cecb43099c","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgPTYmitiAFdfREcqcs6b","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4310,"cache_read_input_tokens":692057,"output_tokens":1613,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":591},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4310,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1613,"cache_read_input_tokens":692057,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4310,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4310},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":5599,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","serverClassifierRequest":"f626446e-6a5f-4e42-8920-a12c059ccb86","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CgPTYmKpbAvto3Jx7BLmG","requestRef":"b3971c3a-4c07-4510-89f3-a60e882120c2","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"9dbe027c-d9b1-4089-b8b9-cca50a87929f","timestamp":"2026-10-25T18:51:12.596Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","version":"2.1.308","gitBranch":"perf/shared-index-on-growth","slug":"purring-snuggling-matsumoto"}
{"parentUuid":"9dbe027c-d9b1-4089-b8b9-cca50a87929f","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgPTYmitiAFdfREcqcs6b","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, the oracle is the right source of labels. BM25 is not the right algorithm, though, and the simplest scoring method will probably be enough.\n\n**Using the oracle for labels.** It gives a verdict per prop (matches, less precise, wrong, opaque), so we can record, for every prop, the signals we had and whether the oracle disagreed. The score is then \"how often did the oracle disagree when we had this combination of signals?\" That is an empirical rate, not a guess.\n\n**Why not BM25**\n- BM25 ranks documents against a text query by term frequency and rarity. Our task is predicting whether a type answer is wrong from a handful of discrete signals. That is classification with a risk estimate, not text retrieval.\n- The one idea worth borrowing is rarity weighting: an unusual construct (a type shape rarely seen across corpora) is probably riskier. I'd use that as a feature, not use BM25 itself.\n\n**What would work, from simplest up**\n1. **A risk table** (my recommendation to start). Group props by their signals: how the type was found (declaration, evaluated, shortcut, opaque), which diagnostic codes touch them, and the detection rule. For each group, store the observed oracle-disagreement rate with smoothing, since many groups will be small. It is tiny, explainable, ships as data, and each routing decision can say why.\n2. **Logistic regression or a small tree** on the same features plus shape features (type nesting depth, number of unresolved names, generic arity, source package). Useful if the table leaves a lot of routed work that was unnecessary.\n3. **Calibration** (Platt or isotonic) so a score of 0.2 really means about 20% disagreement, which the routing threshold depends on.\n\n**Cautions from our data**\n- **Positives are rare.** The oracle says \"wrong\" on only 0.0–0.3% of props, a few hundred in total. \"Less precise\" is far more common (about 8–15% on some corpora), so I'd train on a softer label that combines the two and report both.\n- **Only five corpora.** Evaluate by leaving one corpus out, or the score will just learn the corpora. Chakra alone is 470,000 shared props and would dominate, so weight by corpus.\n- **The oracle has known blind spots** (depth beyond 3 treated like opaque, objects compared by field names). Labels inherit those. Confidently wrong answers with no diagnostic (like the antd union-props bug) are what the score must catch, so check specifically how many of those it would have flagged.\n- **Free labels later:** once `tsc` answers on demand, every routed component gives a new label, so the table can keep improving.\n\n**How to judge it:** plot the share of oracle disagreements caught against the share of components sent to `tsc`. I'd target catching most disagreements while routing under about 10%, which is roughly today's fallback rate. If a score can't beat the existing \"has a warning or error\" rule on that curve, it isn't worth the complexity.\n\nThat is exactly the measurement fork I proposed. Want me to start it with the risk-table method first? Running now: `antd-gaps` and `deps-default`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4310,"cache_read_input_tokens":692057,"output_tokens":1613,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":591},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4310,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1613,"cache_read_input_tokens":692057,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4310,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4310},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"f626446e-6a5f-4e42-8920-a12c059ccb86","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgPTYmKpbAvto3Jx7BLmG","requestRef":"b3971c3a-4c07-4510-89f3-a60e882120c2","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"57ce1f16-2c05-4516-a545-ed8358dc91d7","timestamp":"2026-10-25T18:51:20.301Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","version":"2.1.308","gitBranch":"perf/shared-index-on-growth","slug":"purring-snuggling-matsumoto"}
