mponents would later be answered on demand by a warm tsc (TS 7.1) in an `auto` mode. Signals available per prop/component: how the type was produced (declaration read / structurally evaluated / hand shortcut in crates/core/src/known.rs e.g. VariantProps / opaque or unresolved named reference / composes), diagnostics with codes affecting the component (UNRESOLVABLE_IMPORT, OPAQUE_TYPE, INDEXED_ACCESS_OPAQUE, TEMPLATE_LITERAL_OPAQUE, GENERIC_ARGUMENT_MISMATCH, UNKNOWN, CIRCULAR_REFERENCE, max-depth), DetectionRule evidence of the component (crates/core/src/types/collected.rs; name-based wrapper heuristics are the shakiest), type shape (nesting depth, union size, generic arity, number of named refs), source (project vs dependency package), whether defaults/params involved. Labels: the oracle (apps/validate/src/oracle*.ts, `python3 scripts/parity.py oracle` / `scripts/equivalence.py --oracle`, baseline in scripts/oracle-baseline.json; per-prop verdicts: match, less precise, wrong, opaque; wrong is only 0.0-0.3% of props so use label = wrong OR less-precise as the soft label and report wrong alone too; know its blind spots: depth>3 and opaque treated alike, objects compared by field names). Do: (1) add a small offline exporter (an example in crates/core/examples/ or a script over `extract --json` + the oracle's per-prop output; keep it tiny and committed under scripts/ or apps/validate if useful; no shipped-code changes) producing one row per (corpus, component, prop) with the signals above and the oracle label, for the five corpora in /Users/gabe/.claude/jobs/ba6a68d6/tmp/bench-corpus with --load-dependencies --html-attributes full; (2) analysis in python (-I): the empirical disagreement rate per signal and per signal combination (a smoothed risk table; Beta prior), then a simple logistic regression / small tree for comparison; evaluate by leave-one-corpus-out; weight corpora so Chakra's 470k props do not dominate; (3) the key chart: share of oracle disagreements (wrong; wrong+less-precise) caught vs share of COMPONENTS routed (a component is routed if any prop's risk exceeds a threshold), for (a) today's rule (any warning/error diagnostic affects the component = the addon's fallback rule), (b) the risk table, (c) the model; report at routed budgets 2%, 5%, 10%, 20%; (4) list the confidently-wrong cases (oracle wrong, no diagnostic, high confidence) and what signal would have caught them (examples from the session: union-props branch, VariantProps by bare name); (5) conclusion: does a score beat the existing diagnostic rule at <=10% routed? what thresholds? what extra signals should the resolver emit (e.g. a per-prop provenance field) to make the score better; propose the data model (small ordered enum levels + how component confidence aggregates) and the ADR outline, but do not implement it. Output: report at /Users/gabe/.claude/jobs/ba6a68d6/tmp/confidence-score.md and a summary in your final message with the key table. If the exporter/script is clean and small open a PR (base main) with it plus docs/confidence-score.md findings; `just lint doc-check machete typos` exit code read directly. Time-box ~70 tool calls. Commits end with Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/REDACTED; PR body ends with https://claude.ai/code/REDACTED; never push main/force-push/merge; /usr/bin/git with sandbox off, one plain git command per call.","outputFile":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-caliper--claude-worktrees-release-0-2-0/ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675/tasks/a9a3030cd4e625228.output","canReadOutputFile":true,"canContinueAgent":true},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"2f6c890c-8e89-4a89-ad17-07091c794ed0","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0","root":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0","branch":"chore/reset-equivalence-allow-list","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_norma