{"parentUuid":"a978a3fa-3dfc-4c9d-9c76-e51fdc11d6ae","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgR3UuZHV2oUHrdKvYdHu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1448,"cache_read_input_tokens":807277,"output_tokens":3321,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":746},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1448,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3321,"cache_read_input_tokens":807277,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1448,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1448},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":6473,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","serverClassifierRequest":"75fcebfe-a74b-4102-b106-a49a3211641d","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CgR3Uu12y3nfPqKhC7BZ4","requestRef":"34c440eb-0eb5-4941-b36a-dd413bf142b9","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"1b85d424-69da-443b-9779-683913380544","timestamp":"2026-10-26T14:56:50.266Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","version":"2.1.308","gitBranch":"ci/repair-gates","slug":"purring-snuggling-matsumoto"}
{"parentUuid":"1b85d424-69da-443b-9779-683913380544","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgR3UuZHV2oUHrdKvYdHu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, the add-on exists, but it only covers part of this. There are two different things in the repo:\n\n- **The TS 7.1 backend** (PR #154, the `semantic` and `tsc-client` crates): the opt-in `--experimental-semantic-types` flag. It runs a `tsc` process over the **whole project** and falls back to the structural resolver with a `SEMANTIC_BACKEND` warning if `tsc` fails. That's the 0.5–9.3 s mode.\n- **The Storybook addon**: it answers one component per story through the lazy session, and hands degraded ones to Storybook's docgen. It doesn't use `tsc` at all.\n\nWhat's missing is the join: asking a warm `tsc` for **just the component a story asks for**. Let me start it. It's a measurement-first fork, because the confidence-score result means we can't predict which components to route."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1448,"cache_read_input_tokens":807277,"output_tokens":3321,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":746},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1448,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3321,"cache_read_input_tokens":807277,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1448,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1448},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"75fcebfe-a74b-4102-b106-a49a3211641d","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgR3Uu12y3nfPqKhC7BZ4","requestRef":"34c440eb-0eb5-4941-b36a-dd413bf142b9","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"c9650e42-6803-454f-828e-307d1ddb81b7","timestamp":"2026-10-26T14:56:52.267Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","version":"2.1.308","gitBranch":"ci/repair-gates","slug":"purring-snuggling-matsumoto"}
{"parentUuid":"c9650e42-6803-454f-828e-307d1ddb81b7","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgR3UuZHV2oUHrdKvYdHu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DWRiykF7L3JHH36CxB6h3u","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"On-demand TS 7.1 resolution per requested component","subagent_type":"fork","isolation":"worktree","name":"ts-on-demand","prompt":"Track AM: on-demand TS 7.1 resolution for the component a story asks for. Do NOT remove worktrees (others, including Codex, use the repo). Branch off origin/main (fetch first); PR base `main`; stack PRs only on your own earlier PRs. Context: today the TS 7.1 backend (PR #154: crates/semantic + crates/tsc-client, `--experimental-semantic-types [--tsc-path P]`, config keys experimentalSemanticTypes/tscPath, ADR 0006/0007, docs/type-checker-integration.md, pinned to typescript-beta `7.1.0-beta`) runs a tsc process over the WHOLE project (0.5-9.3 s, Chakra 975 MiB) and falls back to structural with a SEMANTIC_BACKEND warning. The Storybook addon (packages/storybook, lazy session, PR #172/#197/#198-#203) answers ONE component per story through the lazy session (WatchSession::lazy, component(key), NAPI sessionComponentAnswers) and hands degraded components to Storybook's docgen. PR #208 (confidence-score research, scripts/confidence.py, report) showed Caliper-only confidence signals cannot predict where we are wrong (at a 10% component budget: warning rule 23% of missing-prop recall, table 3%, logistic 6%), so DO NOT route by predicted risk: ask the checker for exactly the components that are requested (and cache the answer). Step 1 MEASURE (a spike; commit only a small bench example/script if clean): using crates/semantic + tsc-client (read how it queries: `getTypeAtLocation`-style requests? per-file/per-symbol? batch?), measure on shadcn, antd, mantine, chakra, spectrum (installed corpora /Users/gabe/.claude/jobs/ba6a68d6/tmp/bench-corpus; TS 7.1 beta at apps/validate/node_modules/typescript-beta/bin/tsc): (a) checker startup/cold-program cost for the project (tsconfig), (b) warm per-component query latency distribution (p50/p90/max over 50 random components), (c) memory of the warm tsc process, (d) the accuracy gain per component: share of RDT's props found / oracle agreement when the component comes from the checker vs structural (the earlier run: shadcn 40%->96%, antd 80%->97% component-specific props), (e) whether a per-component query can be answered without checking the whole program (project references / `--noCheck`-style / language-service-style `getTypeOfSymbol` on an already-loaded program: with tsgo the program is built once and queries are cheap). Report the numbers and a go/no-go: what per-story latency can the addon afford (first-story budget ~150 ms, subsequent story ~5-20 ms)? Step 2 DESIGN (ADR 0019-style: look at docs/adr/README.md for the next free number and the adr skill rules; succinct, no hard wrap): an `on-demand` semantic mode: the lazy session owns a warm tsc client started lazily in the background on first request (or at session start behind an option); `component(key)` returns the structural answer immediately unless the option says wait; contract choices to decide with numbers: (i) synchronous wait with a timeout (e.g. wait up to N ms for the checker, else structural + an Info diagnostic `SEMANTIC_PENDING`, and the improved answer is cached for the next request), (ii) always structural first then refine for the next request; provenance diagnostic when tsc answered; results cached by (file content hash, tsconfig/lockfile hash, tsc version) in the persistent cache dir (crates/core/src/cache.rs, node_modules/.cache/caliper-docgen) and invalidated on edits via the existing freshness machinery; TS version: optional peer dependency with a SEMVER RANGE and a startup self-check (`tsc --version`) instead of the exact beta pin; failure modes (tsc missing, crash, timeout, version mismatch) degrade to structural with one SEMANTIC_BACKEND warning per session, never fail the story; resource limits (one tsc per session, killed on session close, memory cap). Step 3 IMPLEMENT the smallest vertical slice that proves it end to end: core (the lazy session + semantic crate; no OXC types in the public API; `PipelineOptions`/session options field for the mode: remember a new public field on a non-#[non_exhaustive] struct is a SemVer-visible change: follow how `use_cache`/`load_dependencies` were added), NAPI option, addon option (`semanticTypes: 'off' | 'on-demand'`, default off until stable per ADR 0007/memory note 'TS 7.1 backend is experimental opt-in'), tests with a fake tsc server (crates/semantic/tests/replay* exist) plus one real-tsc integration test gated on the typescript-beta being installed. Measure end to end through the Storybook harness (node packages/storybook/scripts/storybook-parity.mjs --corpora ... --only shadcn,antd --limit 60 --out <file>, after `pnpm --filter caliper-docgen-napi build:napi` and `pnpm --filter caliper-docgen-storybook build`): first story, median story, total, peak RSS, props Storybook lists that we lack, vs structural. Gates: lazy parity unchanged for mode off (byte-identical), `just equivalence` identical with the mode off, `just lint doc-check machete typos` and `cargo nextest run --workspace --exclude caliper-docgen-napi --locked` exit codes read directly (never pipe through tail), pnpm tests for touched packages, SemVer job understood, CI green, changeset (minor). The machine is shared with other forks (a Scorecard bisect is timing on it): repeat measurements and report noise. Time-box ~110 tool calls; if Step 1 says no-go, stop after reporting the numbers and the design alternative. Commits end with Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/REDACTED; PR bodies end with https://claude.ai/code/REDACTED; never push main/force-push/merge; /usr/bin/git with sandbox off, one plain git command per call."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"con