{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-10-26T15:12:23.644Z","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>aabbfc817ff6b5c71</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01WgtfWCdLntgGJopkBijcLf</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-caliper--claude-worktrees-release-0-2-0/ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675/tasks/aabbfc817ff6b5c71.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Fix the largest structural type losses against RDT\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Task: re-measure the structural type losses against RDT, then fix the largest ones in `crates/core`. I fixed one, member reads on intersections, and measured the rest. The rest-parameter loss is a real one I scoped for follow-up instead of starting.\n\n**PR:** https://github.com/orin-dx/caliper/pull/230, base `main`, independent of the other open PRs. Commit `019ba700` on `fix/type-losses`: `crates/core/src/resolver/evaluate.rs`, `crates/core/tests/intersection_members.rs`, `scripts/equivalence-allow.json`, `.changeset/read-members-of-intersections.md`. I did not watch its CI.\n\n**Fix.** `member_uncached` had no arm for intersections, so any member read on `A &amp; B` returned nothing. Chakra's `Tokens = { spacing: SpacingToken; … } &amp; { [token: string]: never }` therefore left every style prop as `Tokens[\"spacing\"]`. It now reads the property from the constituents that declare it, as TypeScript does. One declarer gives its type, several give their intersection, none stays opaque with the existing diagnostic. A reduction such as `string &amp; (\"x\" | \"y\")` to `\"x\" | \"y\"` is not done, and a test pins that.\n\n**Loss table on current `main`** (`--load-dependencies`, occurrences; the 0.4.0 baseline is in parentheses where it differs)\n\n| Pattern | Mantine | Chakra | shadcn | antd | Spectrum |\n| --- | --: | --: | --: | --: | --: |\n| member read left as `X[\"k\"]` | 524 (6,542) | 148,646 → **20,831** (378,118) | 129 | 138 | 81 |\n| rest parameter lost | 380 | 15,182 | 12 | 140 | 12 |\n| readonly lost | 0 | 357,474 | 101 | 26 | 33 |\n| template literal left | 235 (469) | 1 | 11 | 0 | 0 |\n| conditional left as source | 7 (226) | 0 | 4 | 0 | 0 |\n| unbound type parameter | 31 | 1 | 27 | 2 | 1,399 |\n\nThe Chakra counts repeat the same style props across 600 components, so I also counted distinct pairs. Member reads went from 161 distinct to 27, and every other cell is unchanged by this fix.\n\n**Gates.** Equivalence against `main` on all five corpora is identical on shadcn, antd and Spectrum. Chakra's 128,496 prop-type changes (241 distinct props) are all the intended `Tokens[\"…\"]` rewrites, and Mantine swaps one informational diagnostic for the next unevaluated conditional it now reaches. The allow-list names those 241 props and the two diagnostics, with no bare wildcards. Lazy parity passes over the fixtures and all five corpora. Lint exits 0 and 903 tests pass.\n\n**Oracle.** Wrong-prop rate is 0.00% on the fixtures, Mantine, Chakra, shadcn and antd, and 0.01% on Spectrum, improved or equal on every set. The gate prints four \"components matched fell\" failures (fixtures 82.50 → 77.50, Mantine 89.73 → 89.46, antd 59.68 → 59.35, Spectrum 41.13 → 40.85). They compare against a stale baseline, and equivalence shows no components added or removed here, so I read them as not caused by this change. I did not run the oracle on `main` itself to prove it.\n\n**Measurement artifacts, not product bugs.**\n- Spectrum's 1,399 \"unbound type parameter\" hits are its breakpoint keys `S`, `M`, `L` matching the single-capital-letter regex in `scripts/parity.py`.\n- Many \"member read\" and \"rest parameter\" hits are RDT printing an alias name (`DOMRenderFunction&lt;…&gt;`, `Responsive&lt;string&gt;`) or truncating a union with `…`, where ours is a correct, longer form. The patterns in `scripts/parity.py` could be tightened.\n\n**Not done (the real remaining loss).** Rest and optional parameters are dropped from function types: `(...args: any[]) =&gt; void` prints as `() =&gt; void`, `(a: string, ...rest: number[])` as `(a: string)`, and `(a?: string)` as `(a: string)`. This is antd's 102 distinct hits and Mantine's rest cases. `FunctionParam` has only `name` and `param_type`, so the fix needs new `optional` and `rest` fields carried from the extractor through the collected types and cache schema to the output, the RDT serializer and the JSON schema. That is a `SCHEMA_VERSION` bump with an ADR note under ADR 0017, and should be its own PR.\n\n**Smaller leads.** `readonly` is lost on 540 distinct Chakra types and `RefObject` shown as `Ref` on 16 shadcn ones; neither was investigated. The template-literal and conditional categories are down to a few distinct cases after earlier fixes.\n\n**Merge order:** #230 is independent, so it can merge any time.\n\n**Housekeeping.** The loss-pair analyzer is at `/Users/gabe/.claude/jobs/ba6a68d6/tmp/ak/distinct.py`, and the before and after JSON files are in the same directory.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>908739</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>84</tool_uses><duration_ms>1159085</duration_ms></usage>\n<worktree><worktreePath>/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/agent-aabbfc817ff6b5c71</worktreePath><worktreeBranch>worktree-agent-aabbfc817ff6b5c71</worktreeBranch></worktree>\n</task-notification>"}
