sets): read docs/adr/README.md and the adr skill rules, write a short ADR (next free number; succinct, no hard wrap) or an amendment note explaining the new fields and the version bump; keep the interning (PropType::structural_hash must include the new fields so distinct signatures don't share). Also check type printing everywhere a function type is rendered (alias display, `raw_string`, `display_string`, the Storybook arg-types conversion in packages/storybook/src/arg-types.ts for function controls), and `readonly`: the loss table showed `readonly lost` on 540 distinct Chakra types, 101 shadcn, 26 antd, 33 Spectrum, and `RefObject shown as Ref` on 16 shadcn: investigate each briefly; fix `readonly` for array/tuple types if the output model can carry it cheaply in the same schema bump, otherwise write down what it would take. Gates: exact-value tests (extractor, resolver, serializer round trips, cache golden test with schema bump), `just equivalence` showing only intended function-type differences with NARROW allow-list entries in your PR (a fork is making the list empty on main and a validator rejects bare `*` category entries), the oracle (`just equivalence --oracle`) no regression (wrong ~0.0%), lazy parity, the napi tests and the addon/vite-plugin tests (`pnpm --filter caliper-docgen-storybook test`, vite plugin), cargo semver (a pub type change is visible: use the repo's convention), `just lint doc-check machete typos` and `cargo nextest run --workspace --exclude caliper-docgen-napi --locked` exit codes read directly (never pipe through tail), CI green. HOUSE RULES: changeset = one short, consumer-facing paragraph, friendly and scannable, starting with the OUTCOME in the user's terms then the action/cost if needed (here: readers must be updated together, schemaVersion moves; say it plainly), no heading, no list marker, no bold lead-in, no internal names, one line per paragraph; commits: imperative subject, at most 1-2 sentences of why, no list of what changed; 