mote-settings-helper-consent\",\"... and 144 more (truncated for prompt size)\"]}}\n\n - You should always default to running commands within the sandbox. Do NOT attempt to set `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true` unless:\n  - The user *explicitly* asks you to bypass sandbox\n  - A specific command just failed and you see evidence of sandbox restrictions causing the failure. Note that commands can fail for many reasons unrelated to the sandbox (missing files, wrong arguments, network issues, etc.).\n - Evidence of sandbox-caused failures includes:\n  - \"Operation not permitted\" errors for file/network operations\n  - Access denied to specific paths outside allowed directories\n  - Network connection failures to non-whitelisted hosts\n  - Unix socket connection errors\n - When you see evidence of sandbox-caused failure:\n  - If the `<sandbox_violations>` block names a denied host, re-run the command with that host in its `allowed_domains` (auto mode); otherwise retry with `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true` directly rather than asking in prose first — the retry itself goes through the permission gate (a user prompt, or the auto-mode classifier when auto mode is active)\n  - Briefly explain what sandbox restriction likely caused the failure. Be sure to mention that the user can use the `/sandbox` command to manage restrictions.\n - A sandbox denial on a credential, a file or a host that the task does not involve is the boundary above at work: tell the user rather than retrying with `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true`.\n - Treat each command you execute with `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true` individually. Even if you have recently run a command with this setting, you should default to running future commands within the sandbox.\n - Do not suggest adding sensitive paths like ~/.bashrc, ~/.zshrc, ~/.ssh/*, or credential files to the sandbox allowlist.\n - Network egress goes through a filtering proxy. Attempt requests and read the error rather than predicting whether a host is reachable; denied connections are reported in a `<sandbox_violations>` block explaining the reason. In auto mode, list the hosts a command needs beyond that in its `allowed_domains` — a denied host named in the block goes there on the re-run.\n - If a command fails to bind or listen on a local port with \"Operation not permitted\" (EPERM), local port binding is off in this sandbox. Treat it as the sandbox-caused failure described above, and tell the user that `sandbox.network.allowLocalBinding: true` in their settings (it applies without a restart) allows it without leaving the sandbox.\n - Opening an app, file or URL with `open`, or scripting another app with `osascript`, is blocked inside this sandbox (macOS Launch Services and Apple Events are off) and fails with errors such as -10822 (kLSServerCommunicationErr), -54, -600 or \"LSOpenURLsWithRole() failed\". That failure is the sandbox, not a problem with what you built; do not retry it with `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true`. Tell the user the sandbox blocked it and give them the exact command to run themselves.\n - For temporary files, always use the `$TMPDIR` environment variable. TMPDIR is automatically set to the correct sandbox-writable directory in sandbox mode. Do NOT use `/tmp` directly - use `$TMPDIR` instead.\n - If a clipboard utility such as `pbcopy`, `xclip`, or `wl-copy` fails inside the sandbox and the user wants the text on their clipboard, put the text in a fenced code block in your response and tell them to run `/copy` (it copies from outside the sandbox; when the picker appears they can select just that block), rather than writing a file for them to copy manually.\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","version":"2.1.308","gitBranch":"feat/function-param-flags","slug":"purring-snuggling-matsumoto"}
{"parentUuid":"a1ea0d63-8fb1-41f8-a34d-847440d0364a","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14993341 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"77159344-3162-41ce-a5c5-1652cce6423e","timestamp":"2026-10-26T15:44:55.213Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14993341 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","version":"2.1.308","gitBranch":"feat/function-param-flags","slug":"purring-snuggling-matsumoto"}
{"parentUuid":"77159344-3162-41ce-a5c5-1652cce6423e","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"output_style","style":"Concise","turnReminder":"Be concise: lead with the result, skip preamble and narration, keep only what the user needs."},"type":"attachment","uuid":"d31cfb67-9eab-418f-b865-8fb03614cf1c","timestamp":"2026-10-26T15:44:55.213Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nConcise output style is active. Be concise: lead with the result, skip preamble and narration, keep only what the user needs.\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","version":"2.1.308","gitBranch":"feat/function-param-flags","slug":"purring-snuggling-matsumoto"}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","hash":"247b0c3a60996ffbcb9ec2745e01184ce203a1fcc2954ffdb63750b4dc7aba6e","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Yes, they matter: `children`, `aria-*` and the DOM event handlers are what people actually use. The checker pass was dropping them, and the fix is to apply one policy to both engines instead of letting the checker decide.\n\n**What happened.** In #232 the checker's answer left out every prop that `@types/react` declares. The fork patched that by keeping the structural pass's inherited props. It still loses 3 on shadcn and 5 on antd, which are own props that reach the component through `PropsWithChildren`, `children` for example. Those have no inheritance parent, so the patch didn't catch them.\n\n**The fix I'd make.**\n1. **Merge by name, not by parent.** After the checker answers, keep every structural prop the checker didn't return, with its structural type. Where both have a prop, the checker's type wins. That covers `children`, `className`, ARIA and handlers whichever way they were declared.\n2. **Stop the checker side from filtering React declarations at all.** The `htmlAttributes` setting (`curated`, `full`, `none`) should be the only thing deciding which DOM attributes show up, applied after the merge. Then both engines follow the same rule and a user's setting means the same thing in either mode.\n3. **Test it** with a component using `PropsWithChildren`, one with `HTMLAttributes` and one with an own `children`, in on-demand and structural modes, expecting identical sets under `full`.\n\n**A decision for you: `ref` and `key`.** Today we leave out both, and RDT lists them (they were 24 of the props the site said we miss). `key` is never a real prop at runtime, but in React 19 `ref` is a normal prop on function components. I'd include `ref` for function components and keep omitting `key`. Say if you'd rather match RDT on both.\n\nI'll resume the `ts-on-demand` fork for this, since it has the context. It will also check whether the default structural `curated` mode drops any React-declared props that a story needs."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CojPS78b7vrjsqHYxb8qao","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"ts-on-demand","message":"Follow-up from the user: we must not omit @types/react-declared props at all in the on-demand mode (children, aria-*, DOM handlers, className, style, id, etc.). Your inherited-props patch still loses 3 shadcn and 5 antd props (own props reaching the component via PropsWithChildren etc., with no parent). Fix generally, as a new commit on PR #232's branch (feat/semantic-on-demand; it is open, unmerged; stacked work stays on that branch): (1) merge by NAME not by parent: after the checker answers, keep every structural prop the checker did not return (structural type, provenance noted); where both have a prop the checker's type wins; (2) remove any filtering of @types/react-declared props on the semantic side (crates/semantic): the `htmlAttributes` setting (curated / full / none, the same policy and curated list the structural path uses in crates/core/src/react_types.rs and resolver/html.rs) is applied AFTER the merge, once, for both engines, so a user's setting means the same thing in either mode; (3) tests: a component with PropsWithChildren<P>, one extending React.HTMLAttributes<HTMLDivElement>, and one with an own `children?: ReactNode`, in structural and on-demand modes with htmlAttributes full: identical prop NAME sets (the checker only improves types/optionality); curated and none behave per their definition in both modes; (4) re-run the shadcn/antd harness (node packages/storybook/scripts/storybook-parity.mjs --corpora /Users/gabe/.claude/jobs/ba6a68d6/tmp/bench-corpus --only shadcn,antd --limit 60 --out <file>; build binding+addon first) and report 'props Storybook lists that we lack' (target 0 on both) plus first/median story; also check whether the DEFAULT structural curated mode drops any React-declared props a story needs (children/aria/handlers) and report. `ref`/`key`: the user's call is pending; do not change them yet but make the policy a single named place so it is a one-line change (propose: include `ref` for function components in React 19 semantics, omit `key`). Same house rules as before (consumer-facing friendly changeset, small commits, push before finishing, lint and tests with exit codes read directly, CI green). Update the PR body and the ADR 0022 text if the contract changes.","summary":"Ask ts-on-demand to keep all React props by name merge"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
