ond that in its `allowed_domains` — a denied host named in the block goes there on the re-run.\n - If a command fails to bind or listen on a local port with \"Operation not permitted\" (EPERM), local port binding is off in this sandbox. Treat it as the sandbox-caused failure described above, and tell the user that `sandbox.network.allowLocalBinding: true` in their settings (it applies without a restart) allows it without leaving the sandbox.\n - Opening an app, file or URL with `open`, or scripting another app with `osascript`, is blocked inside this sandbox (macOS Launch Services and Apple Events are off) and fails with errors such as -10822 (kLSServerCommunicationErr), -54, -600 or \"LSOpenURLsWithRole() failed\". That failure is the sandbox, not a problem with what you built; do not retry it with `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true`. Tell the user the sandbox blocked it and give them the exact command to run themselves.\n - For temporary files, always use the `$TMPDIR` environment variable. TMPDIR is automatically set to the correct sandbox-writable directory in sandbox mode. Do NOT use `/tmp` directly - use `$TMPDIR` instead.\n - If a clipboard utility such as `pbcopy`, `xclip`, or `wl-copy` fails inside the sandbox and the user wants the text on their clipboard, put the text in a fenced code block in your response and tell them to run `/copy` (it copies from outside the sandbox; when the picker appears they can select just that block), rather than writing a file for them to copy manually.\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","version":"2.1.308","gitBranch":"ci/triage-parity-regressions","slug":"purring-snuggling-matsumoto"}
{"parentUuid":"bbb45e3e-d141-4b57-b7b2-00cb6125620d","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14971235 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"e6bddb85-5f78-4492-ab27-5f33b4e2356b","timestamp":"2026-10-26T16:04:30.675Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14971235 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","version":"2.1.308","gitBranch":"ci/triage-parity-regressions","slug":"purring-snuggling-matsumoto"}
{"parentUuid":"e6bddb85-5f78-4492-ab27-5f33b4e2356b","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"output_style","style":"Concise","turnReminder":"Be concise: lead with the result, skip preamble and narration, keep only what the user needs."},"type":"attachment","uuid":"dfeefc62-1d50-450c-848b-032a5240eeeb","timestamp":"2026-10-26T16:04:30.676Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nConcise output style is active. Be concise: lead with the result, skip preamble and narration, keep only what the user needs.\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","version":"2.1.308","gitBranch":"ci/triage-parity-regressions","slug":"purring-snuggling-matsumoto"}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","hash":"c8fa8005930b58309722238198f2e3fa769234f5e9fe68e7c7866256841a76bf","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"PR #234 is a draft, stacked on #224's branch, and it's only partly done. It fixed one real regression and one misleading metric, and left a second regression and the baseline refresh undone.\n\n**Fixed**\n- **Mantine's `component` prop** (25 props) printed `C | ElementType`, which leaked a call signature's own type parameter. It now prints `ElementType`, and the pipeline test asserts the exact value.\n- **Spectrum's \"unbound type parameter\" count** was inflated because the pattern matched the breakpoint keys `\"S\" | \"M\" | \"L\"`. The pattern now ignores string literals, with a test.\n\n**Triage table**\n\n| Metric | Class | Action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Mantine `component` prop text | real regression | fixed; one narrow allow-list entry that must be removed after merge |\n| Spectrum unbound type parameter | scored as a loss by a naive pattern | pattern tightened |\n| base-ui `MenuTrigger` detection | **real regression, from #209 (Codex)** | not fixed |\n| Chakra utility-type growth | acceptable | none |\n| Spectrum, antd, Mantine oracle numbers | stale baseline | refresh later |\n\n**The `MenuTrigger` regression is real.** `MenuTrigger` returns `renderByProp(...)`, a same-file helper annotated `React.ReactElement`, and #209's narrower call evidence stopped counting it as a component. The fix is to treat calls to same-file element-returning helpers as element evidence in `extractor/evidence.rs`. The fork ran out of time before doing it.\n\n**The repaired Parity workflow isn't fully working yet.** The dispatch on the branch (run 40355403892) failed three ways:\n1. `Allow-list is empty` fails because of the one allow-list entry, which is intended while the PR is open.\n2. The oracle step fails because `apps/validate/node_modules/typescript-beta` isn't installed in the dispatch run.\n3. The equivalence step is given a path, `$RUNNER_TEMP/caliper-base`, where `git rev-parse` needs a commit.\n\n2 and 3 look like #224 workflow defects that only show up on dispatch. So #224 is correct for the install, the allow-list guard and the PR speed gate, but the weekly Parity workflow still needs these two fixes before it can be trusted.\n\nI'm resuming two forks:\n- **`gates`:** fix defects 2 and 3 in #224, and confirm with a clean dispatch run.\n- **`parity-triage`:** fix `MenuTrigger` with exact tests, then refresh the baselines in their own commit once the workflow runs clean."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VULr9eacBqZPxjxTHqBDAB","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"gates","message":"Follow-up on your PR #224 (branch ci/repair-gates; keep committing to it). The triage fork dispatched the weekly Parity workflow on its branch (run 40355403892) and two steps fail for reasons that look like defects in your workflow changes that only show on dispatch: (1) the oracle step fails with FileNotFoundError on apps/validate/node_modules/typescript-beta/package.json: the dispatch run does not install apps/validate's dependencies including the typescript-beta alias (check parity.yml's install steps vs what the oracle needs: `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` at the root, the alias dependency in apps/validate/package.json, and whether the corepack/COREPACK_HOME manifest change or a missing step skips it); (2) the equivalence step is given `$RUNNER_TEMP/caliper-base` (a path) where `git rev-parse` needs a commit: scripts/equivalence.py takes a git ref OR an executable; make the argument handling accept an existing executable path first (os.path.isfile + executable) before trying `rev-parse`, with a unit test in scripts/test_equivalence.py, or fix what parity.yml passes. Fix both, dispatch it yourself (`gh workflow run parity.yml --ref ci/repair-gates`, watch with `gh run watch`) and confirm the Install, Allow-list-empty, Measure/gate, Equivalence and Oracle steps reach their real pass/fail verdicts (the accuracy gate may still fail on baseline drift: that is the triage fork's job; report exactly which steps pass). Same house rules (friendly consumer-facing changeset only if shipped text changes; CI/tooling needs none; small commits; push before finishing; lint with exit codes read directly).","summary":"Ask gates fork to fix the two Parity dispatch defects"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01X7EBRNAnvUvpBXVXGhgvxS","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"parity-triage","message":"Follow-up on PR #234 (branch ci/triage-parity-regressions, base ci/repair-gates). Do next, in order: (1) fix the MenuTrigger regression introduced by #209 (e0b7054f): MenuTrigger returns `renderByProp(...)`, a same-file helper annotated `React.ReactElement`, and #209's narrower call evidence stopped counting it. Fix by shape, not by name, in crates/core/src/extractor/evidence.rs: a call to a same-file function whose declared return type is a React element type (ReactElement / JSX.Element / ReactNode subtype via the existing React-type recognition) counts as element evidence; keep #209's intent (JSX passed to response constructors like `new Response(<div/>)` is NOT render evidence: its constructor-jsx tests must stay green); add exact tests for: the helper-returning-element case, an aliased return type, a helper returning a non-element (negative), and the constructor case. Check base-ui across the other corpora with equivalence for other lost components (list any). (2) The `gates` fork is fixing the two Parity workflow dispatch defects in #224 (oracle needs typescript-beta installed; equivalence given a path instead of a commit): once that lands on ci/repair-gates, merge origin/ci/repair-gates into your branch and re-dispatch `gh workflow run parity.yml --ref ci/triage-parity-regressions`. (3) Then refresh scripts/parity-baseline.json and scripts/oracle-baseline.json in their OWN commit from a clean run of the repaired gates, only for metrics you classified improvement/acceptable, with before/after numbers per corpus in the PR body. (4) Mark #234 ready for review when the Parity workflow reaches real verdicts and passes, or say exactly which gate still fails and why. Remove the allow-list entry only after the PR merges (note it in the PR). House rules as before; commit small, push before finishing.","summary":"Ask parity-triage to fix MenuTrigger and refresh baselines"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","hash":"bec98d67464cb28a247003fa5333a4671908daf973181aea97105e456190a68f","message":{"role":"system","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Bash command sandbox\nBy default, Bash commands run inside an OS-level sandbox (macOS Seatbelt) applied to each command separately, not to the session as a whole; how it is configured in this session is described below.\n\nThe sandbox marks out what this session was given: the directories listed below, the network destinations the task involves, and the credentials the user supplied for it. Treat that as the boundary even where a limit below is not enforced. Commands can reach more than that — credentials and keys elsewhere on this machine, the user's other projects and configuration, sockets that control this machine or other workloads, cloud metadata endpoints — but being reachable does not make them provided; those are the user's, not the task's, unless the user's request calls for them. If the task cannot be finished with what you were given, do what you can and tell the user plainly what is missing instead of finding another way to it; that report is a complete answer.\n\nHow the sandbox is configured in this session:\nFilesystem: {\"read\":{\"denyOnly\":[\"/Users/gabe/.claude/seed-admin\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/bridge-spawn\",\"/private/tmp/claude-501/bash-edit-diff\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/ide\"],\"allowWithinDeny\":[]},\"write\":{\"allowOnly\":[\"/dev/stdout\",\"/dev/stderr\",\"/dev/null\",\"/dev/tty\",\"/dev/dtracehelper\",\"/dev/autofs_nowait\",\"/tmp/claude\",\"/private/tmp/claude\",\"/Users/gabe/.npm/_logs\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/debug\",\".\",\"$TMPDIR\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.git\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/agent-accbf15a5e0d1dd07\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/agent-a77265bdad974924a\"],\"denyWithinAllow\":[\"/Users/gabe/.claude/settings.json\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktre