{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","hash":"2fa34e370fca9c27d40cf16e253724586650d2ced802c619c25e4e081a8cfaef","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Yes, keep going. Accuracy against RDT is the product, and refreshing the baselines over unclassified movements would hide regressions. I'm resuming `parity-triage` with a clearer plan."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TcTtKquY5hxfeHiKaVZaaF","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"parity-triage","message":"Yes: keep triaging in this task; extended time-box of ~100 more tool calls. Plan, in order: (1) The dispatched run's verdicts may predate your MenuTrigger fix (68c62e1f): first re-dispatch on the CURRENT branch tip and use only that run's numbers. Add a `corpora` input to the parity workflow_dispatch (e.g. 'chakra,spectrum') in your PR only if it saves you rounds (gates owns parity.yml on ci/repair-gates; merge it first; keep the edit small). (2) Name the second lost fixture component: build the baseline-commit binary (the commit where scripts/oracle-baseline.json was last updated: `git log -- scripts/oracle-baseline.json`) in a scratch dir, extract the fixtures with both builds (`--format rdt`/json, the same flags the oracle uses), diff component lists; bisect the introducing PR (#209 narrowed evidence is the prime suspect for Spectrum's component drops too: check the Spectrum 77.20 -> 76.19 'components matched' list against it with the fixed build; the MenuTrigger fix may already recover some). (3) Classify EVERY remaining Parity-gate movement with before/after examples: Chakra 'utility type left as written' 1 -> 1,127 and 'unreduced & string' 0 -> 40; 'rest parameter lost' (the loose `...` pattern also matches TypeScript's own '... N more ...' elision: tighten the pattern in scripts/parity.py to a strict `...name:` match with a test, as the type-loss fork found); 'readonly lost' (a known unimplemented follow-up of PR #233/ADR 0022: classify as known-gap, not a regression, unless it grew because of a change); Mantine 'member read left as X[k]' 6,542 -> 6,614 (find the 72 new props); Spectrum same text 67.42 -> 63.03; shadcn matched 95.29 -> 95.23; antd 'typeof left' 7 -> 8. Fix real regressions by shape with exact-value tests, narrow allow-list entries only in your PR. (4) Refresh scripts/parity-baseline.json and scripts/oracle-baseline.json in their own commit ONLY for rows classified improvement/acceptable/known-gap, with a per-row table in the PR body; leave rows that are real unfixed regressions failing and list them. (5) Mark #234 ready only if the Parity workflow passes on the final tip; otherwise say exactly which gate fails and why. House rules as before (consumer-facing friendly changeset only for shipped behavior; small commits; push before finishing).","summary":"Tell parity-triage to continue and classify remaining rows"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","hash":"f9778e6b7e54f2f68dfcb8895d46574e4fce9bff46fcd943f06a221ba2602543","message":{"role":"system","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Bash command sandbox\nBy default, Bash commands run inside an OS-level sandbox (macOS Seatbelt) applied to each command separately, not to the session as a whole; how it is configured in this session is described below.\n\nThe sandbox marks out what this session was given: the directories listed below, the network destinations the task involves, and the credentials the user supplied for it. Treat that as the boundary even where a limit below is not enforced. Commands can reach more than that — credentials and keys elsewhere on this machine, the user's other projects and configuration, sockets that control this machine or other workloads, cloud metadata endpoints — but being reachable does not make them provided; those are the user's, not the task's, unless the user's request calls for them. If the task cannot be finished with what you were given, do what you can and tell the user plainly what is missing instead of finding another way to it; that report is a complete answer.\n\nHow the sandbox is configured in this session:\nFilesystem: {\"read\":{\"denyOnly\":[\"/Users/gabe/.claude/seed-admin\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/bridge-spawn\",\"/private/tmp/claude-501/bash-edit-diff\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/ide\"],\"allowWithinDeny\":[]},\"write\":{\"allowOnly\":[\"/dev/stdout\",\"/dev/stderr\",\"/dev/null\",\"/dev/tty\",\"/dev/dtracehelper\",\"/dev/autofs_nowait\",\"/tmp/claude\",\"/private/tmp/claude\",\"/Users/gabe/.npm/_logs\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/debug\",\".\",\"$TMPDIR\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.git\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/agent-ad5269bcad04ba5a7\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/agent-a77265bdad974924a\"],\"denyWithinAllow\":[\"/Users/gabe/.claude/settings.json\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0/.claude/settings.json\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/settings.local.json\",\"/Library/Application Support/ClaudeCode/managed-settings.json\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0/.claude/settings.local.json\",\"/Library/Application Support/ClaudeCode/managed-settings.d\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0/.claude/skills\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0/.claude/hooks\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/local\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0/.claude/.cc-writes\",\"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/.cc-writes\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/.cc-writes\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/jobs\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/seed-admin\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/daemon\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/bridge-spawn\",\"/private/tmp/claude-501/bash-edit-diff\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/shell-snapshots\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/session-env\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/plugins\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/hooks\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/skills\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/workflows\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/commands\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/agents\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/routines\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/rules\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/output-styles\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/scheduled_tasks.json\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/launch.json\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/CLAUDE.md\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/projects\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/daemon.json\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/policy-limits.json\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/policy-limits.json.signature.json\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/policy-limits.json.signature-iat.json\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/policy-limits.json.stamp.json\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/backups\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/loop.md\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/cowork_plugins\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/dev-mods\",\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-caliper--claude-worktrees-release-0-2-0/ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675/tasks\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/mcp-skill-archives\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/mcp-discovery-cache\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/shares\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/todos\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/statsig\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/logs\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/access-audit\",\"/Users/gabe/.claude/remote-settings-helper-consent\",\"... and 133 more (truncated for prompt size)\"]}}\n\n - You should always default to running commands within the sandbox. Do NOT attempt to set `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true` unless:\n  - The user *explicitly* asks you to bypass sandbox\n  - A specific command just failed and you see evidence of sandbox restrictions causing the failure. Note that commands can fail for many reasons unrelated to the sandbox (missing files, wrong arguments, network issues, etc.).\n - Evidence of sandbox-caused failures includes:\n  - \"Operation not permitted\" errors for file/network operations\n  - Access denied to specific paths outside allowed directories\n  - Network connection failures to non-whitelisted hosts\n  - Unix socket connection errors\n - When you see evidence of sandbox-caused failure:\n  - If the `<sandbox_violations>` block names a denied host, re-run the command with that host in its `allowed_domains` (auto mode); otherwise retry with `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true` directly rather than asking in prose first — the retry itself goes through the permission gate (a user prompt, or the auto-mode classifier when auto mode is active)\n  - Briefly explain what sandbox restriction likely caused the failure. Be sure to mention that the user can use the `/sandbox` command to manage restrictions.\n - A sandbox denial on a credential, a file or a host that the task does not involve is the boundary above at work: tell the user rather than retrying with `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true`.\n - Treat each command you execute with `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true` individually. Even if you have recently run a command with this setting, you should default to running future commands within the sandbox.\n - Do not suggest adding sensitive paths like ~/.bashrc, ~/.zshrc, ~/.ssh/*, or credential files to the sandbox allowlist.\n - Network egress goes through a filtering proxy. Attempt requests and read the error rather than predicting whether a host is reachable; denied connections are reported in a `<sandbox_violations>` block explaining the reason. In auto mode, list the hosts a command needs beyond that in its `allowed_domains` — a denied host named in the block goes there on the re-run.\n - If a command fails to bind or listen on a local port with \"Operation not permitted\" (EPERM), local port binding is off in this sandbox. Treat it as the sandbox-caused failure described above, and tell the user that `sandbox.network.allowLocalBinding: true` in their settings (it applies without a restart) allows it without leaving the sandbox.\n - Opening an app, file or URL with `open`, or scripting another app with `osascript`, is blocked inside this sandbox (macOS Launch Services and Apple Events are off) and fails with errors such as -10822 (kLSServerCommunicationErr), -54, -600 or \"LSOpenURLsWithRole() failed\". That failure is the sandbox, not a problem with what you built; do not retry it with `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true`. Tell the user the sandbox blocked it and give them the exact command to run themselves.\n - For temporary files, always use the `$TMPDIR` environment variable. TMPDIR is automatically set to the correct sandbox-writable directory in sandbox mode. Do NOT use `/tmp` directly - use `$TMPDIR` instead.\n - If a clipboard utility such as `pbcopy`, `xclip`, or `wl-copy` fails inside the sandbox and the user wants the text on their clipboard, put the text in a fenced code block in your response and tell them to run `/copy` (it copies from outside the sandbox; when the picker appears they can select just that block), rather than writing a file for them to copy manually.\n\nThe task tools haven't been used recently. If you're working on tasks that would benefit from tracking progress, consider using TaskCreate to add new tasks and TaskUpdate to update task status (set to in_progress when starting, completed when done). Also consider cleaning up the task list if it has become stale. Only use these if relevant to the current work. This is just a gentle reminder - ignore if not applicable.\n\n\nHere are the existing tasks:\n\n#40. [completed] Type-parameter defaults (fillMissingTypeArguments)\n#41. [completed] Lib types as data: distributive conditionals, intersection_of, one assignability\n#42. [pending] Keep syntax we drop: rest/optional params, readonly, index signatures, RefObject\n#43. [completed] Share identical resolved types (intern PropType in ParsedProp)\n\n<total_tokens>14984978 tokens left</total_tokens>\n\nConcise output style is active. Be concise: lead with the result, skip preamble and narration, keep only what the user needs."}]}}
