{"parentUuid":"551ceb45-99d0-4f41-af00-a0da9eb29d4e","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"9c8768b7-41b1-4e6e-8e89-b8cb1edf01b6","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\n<tools-used-before-summary>Agent, Bash, Edit, EnterWorktree, ExitWorktree, Grep, ListAgents, Read, ReadNotifications, SendMessage, Skill, TaskCreate, TaskStop, TaskUpdate, ToolSearch, WebFetch, WebSearch, Write</tools-used-before-summary>\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   - Long-running goal: make caliper-docgen (Rust/OXC React props extractor; product = accurate AND fast Storybook docs; also agent/MCP use via monokl) better than react-docgen-typescript (RDT) and competitive with react-docgen, via stacked non-draft PRs, with measured evidence. TS 7.1 backend stays an experimental opt-in (off by default).\n   - Latest explicit requests in this segment: \"Let's do everything you identified here please\" (the five adversarial-review items: allow-list guard, PR speed gate + Scorecard bisect, Parity install fix, main-branch measurement, shape-based detection instead of callee names); \"start tackling what is left and the gaps\"; \"We should do the TS on demand\"; \"Let's commit, stack PRs appropriately. Remember to follow our rules for changesets. User focused, friendly, scannable\"; \"FYI we have a homebrew tap repo now right?\"; \"What do we need to do here to allow auto-updating of the brew tap? We have a new action in our actions repo I think.\"; \"We should not be exclude ref and we should dig into why we lost onClick, onFocus, etc. Those are crucial\"; \"FYI I setup the GH app, set the client ID and private key for tap updating\"; \"Seems like we should go to full, it's faster and has a smaller JSON\"; most recent: \"Let's work on making it mergeable\".\n   - Standing constraints: never push to main, never force-push, never merge (user merges); stack PRs non-draft with base = parent head branch when depending on an unmerged PR (never `main` while carrying unmerged parent commits); commits end with `Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/REDACTED`; PR bodies end with `https://claude.ai/code/REDACTED`; changesets per AGENTS.md rules (see analysis); don't modify user's main checkout; remote branch deletion is blocked for me (give commands); one plain git command per call, use `/usr/bin/git`, sandbox off for git/gh; read exit codes directly (never pipe lint/test through tail — that once hid a real lint failure); use `$CLAUDE_JOB_DIR/tmp` (`/Users/gabe/.claude/jobs/ba6a68d6/tmp`) for scratch; measure peak memory as well as time; TS 7.1 stays experimental opt-in; the repo uses pnpm (not yarn; yarn appears only in third-party benchmark corpora). The session-name memory notes: stacked-pr-retargeting (updated: delete_branch_on_merge makes bottom-up merging retarget children; Equivalence job now compares with PR's own base), sandbox-git-and-gh, storybook-is-the-goal, ts71-backend-experimental-opt-in, measure-memory-and-time. Worktree-isolated session: git commands aimed at other worktrees via `-C` or `cd` are refused (use a python script calling git via subprocess for read-only inspection of others; `git worktree remove <path>` works).\n   - Classifier note: a bulk cleanup script run was denied (\"Git Destructive\"); do not work around denials.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - Caliper pipeline: OXC parse → SourceData → GlobalSourceData → ComponentEntry → ExtractionOutput; lazy session (`WatchSession::lazy`, `component(key)`, NAPI `sessionComponentAnswers`), dependency layers (chained, whole-demand background load after 3 growths per Codex's #203), persistent parse cache (content-hash keyed, schema now 24 on main), canonical JSON `SCHEMA_VERSION` (2 on main; 3 in PR #233), ADRs through 0021 on main (0022 claimed by both #232 and #233).\n   - Equivalence tooling: `just equivalence`, `scripts/equivalence.py`, `scripts/equivalence-allow.json` (must be empty on main; validator rejects bare-wildcard entries; `--require-empty-allow`), TS 7.1 oracle (`--oracle`), `scripts/parity.py`, `scripts/scorecard.py`, parity baselines `scripts/parity-baseline.json` / `scripts/oracle-baseline.json` (stale, 0.4.0).\n   - Storybook parity harness: `node packages/storybook/scripts/storybook-parity.mjs --corpora /Users/gabe/.claude/jobs/ba6a68d6/tmp/bench-corpus --only <corpus> --limit N --out <file>` after `pnpm --filter caliper-docgen-napi build:napi` and `pnpm --filter caliper-docgen-storybook build`.\n   - Benchmark corpora at `/Users/gabe/.claude/jobs/ba6a68d6/tmp/bench-corpus` (mantine, chakra-ui, shadcn-ui, ant-design, react-spectrum).\n   - Measured baselines vs RDT (0.4.0 parity baseline, deps on): speed Mantine 24×, Chakra 45×, shadcn 35×, antd 28×, Spectrum 17× (median ~28×); memory 4.2×/1.1×/6.3×/8.1×/3.8×; my adjusted estimates now ~31× median speed and ~4.5× memory (Chakra deps 550 ms→~240 ms, 1,461→~345 MiB). Vs react-docgen: only data point is the site's Node harness (caliper 2.96 s, react-docgen 3.39 s, RDT 10.37 s; Node startup dominates); my estimate 5–15× compute. Recall vs RDT props: Mantine/Chakra/Spectrum 98.6–99.7% with deps; shadcn ~40% (TS 7.1 opt-in ~96%); antd ~80% (opt-in ~97%).\n   - Confidence-score research (Codex #208): Caliper-only signals do not beat the warning rule (at 10% routed: warning rule 23% of missing-prop recall, table 3%, logistic 6%); so on-demand TS asks the checker for the requested component instead of predicting.\n   - TS 7.1 on-demand mode (PR #232, ADR 0022): `semanticTypes: 'on-demand'`, structural answer first, wait `semanticWaitMs` (default 150) for warm tsc, cache late answers, merge by name (checker wins types, structural props kept), `htmlAttributes` applied once via `checker_prop_used` in `crates/core/src/react_types.rs`; `REACT_SPECIAL_PROPS` constant. First answer 82–137 ms (Chakra 703), later stories 21–41 ms (Chakra 534), tsc RSS 157–612 MiB; still exact-pinned `7.1.0-beta`; real-tsc test skipped in CI.\n   - React-props root cause (PR #235): `--format rdt` and addon `docgenInfo` never emitted `notableInherited`, so default curated mode dropped all curated attributes (float-button PurePanel 25 props vs 195 in full); curated table small/hand-written; `Omit<HTMLAttributes>` / `ComponentProps<'div'>` / `PropsWithoutRef` produced no element layer. Widened curated costs CPU +4%…+26%, RSS +6%…+30%, shadcn JSON 17.0 MB vs full 12.2 MB; full as default costs +5% CPU antd, +12–13% RSS antd/Spectrum. User decided: make `full` the default.\n   - Homebrew tap flow: caliper release → `notify-homebrew-tap` action (tap-scoped GitHub App token, `repository_dispatch tool_released` with tool+tag) → tap's `update-formula.yml` verifies npm platform archives, opens formula PR, maintainer approves; daily cron backstop.\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - `.github/workflows/release.yml` (caliper, branch `ci/notify-homebrew-tap`, UNCOMMITTED): inserted after the `Execute verified release` step (before `Summarize receipt`):\n     ```yaml\n           # Callisto tags the release commit; the tap follows the CLI's npm tag.\n           - name: Find the CLI release tag\n             id: cli-tag\n             working-directory: release-source\n             run: |\n               set -euo pipefail\n               git fetch --tags --quiet origin\n               tag=$(git tag --points-at HEAD --list 'npm/caliper-docgen@*' | head -n 1)\n               echo \"tag=${tag}\" >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n               [[ -n \"$tag\" ]] || echo 'This run released no CLI version; the Homebrew tap is not notified.'\n           # The tap also checks daily, so a failed notification never fails the release.\n           - name: Notify the Homebrew tap\n             if: steps.cli-tag.outputs.tag != ''\n             continue-on-error: true\n             uses: orin-dx/actions/notify-homebrew-tap@687b60169775ae9e9270af098a4cabb5e1c2a08e # main\n             with:\n               client-id: ${{ vars.TAP_APP_CLIENT_ID }}\n               private-key: ${{ secrets.TAP_APP_PRIVATE_KEY }}\n               tap-repository: orin-dx/homebrew-tap\n               tool: caliper\n               release-tag: ${{ steps.cli-tag.outputs.tag }}\n     ```\n     `actionlint .github/workflows/release.yml` exit 0. The justfile's `oxfmt --check` covers only `ts_src` and `md_src`, so workflows aren't format-checked by lint.\n   - `scripts/equivalence-allow.json` on main: currently 85 entries with blanket patterns (a fork's PR #224 empties it and adds enforcement).\n   - Scratch scripts in `/Users/gabe/.claude/jobs/ba6a68d6/tmp/`: `verify_branches.py`, `adversarial.py`, `special_case_scan.py`, `backup_failed.py`, `remove_codex_wts.py`, `baseline_table.py`, `cleanup.sh`; patch backups of removed worktrees in `worktree-backups/`.\n   - Key repo docs/files referenced: `AGENTS.md` (changeset/commit rules line 54), `docs/architecture/resolver-type-resolution.md`, `docs/performance.md`, `docs/parity.md`, `docs/adr/` (0016 lazy, 0017 versioned JSON, 0018 interned prop sets, 0019 deps default per #202, 0022 collision), `crates/core/src/react_types.rs`, `crates/core/src/extractor/interface.rs` (variant bag by shape, #225), `.github/workflows/{ci,equivalence,parity,release,scorecard}.yml`.\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - Piping `just lint | tail` hid a real lint failure (PR #172 CI failed three oxlint errors: file too long, `.catch`, async without await). Fixed by moving lookup code into `packages/storybook/src/lookups.ts`, adding `warmUp` private method, making `documentation()` synchronous; since then read exit codes directly and told forks to.\n   - Cleanup bulk script was denied by the auto-mode classifier; did not work around; individual `git worktree remove` / `git branch -D` commands succeeded; sed label bug in cleanup.sh with `/` in branch names (git still deleted).\n   - Forks lost worktrees to outside cleanup (prop-gap, deps-default) and three forks hit the weekly rate limit; Codex finished that work (merged #203–#222); relaunched where needed.\n   - My premise that read recording was unused in one-shot extract was wrong (it drives memo reuse); fixed by #175 (move reads instead of cloning).\n   - Hypothesis that lazy memos were discarded on growth was half right; real cause was context rebuild cloning the index (fixed in #200/#203).\n   - I mis-stated the rest-parameter loss scale (loose `...` pattern matches TS elision); corrected: strict losses only antd (25 hits).\n   - User corrections: \"We don't use yarn, we use pnpm\" (yarn only in third-party corpora); \"Don't compute, we only have a few tokens left\" (use estimates); \"Let's not waste time on the RDT comparison right now\"; \"We should not be ommiting @types/react props at all\"; \"We should probably not exclude ref\"; chose full default.\n   - Parity install real cause (PR #224): `parity.py` put `COREPACK_HOME` inside a repo whose root `package.json` has `\"type\":\"module\"`, so Yarn's CJS bundle loaded as ESM; fixed by writing a CommonJS manifest there. Parity dispatch failures later traced to the allow-list step failing the job and skipping the rest (fixed in #224 via `continue-on-error` on non-main/non-schedule runs).\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - Solved/landed: Scorecard regression was #202's default flip (no node_modules → ~10 ms of failed-resolution walks); fix PR #226 pins scorecard to `--no-load-dependencies` and adds a separate loading gate (1.7×). Variant bags by shape (#225). Intersection member reads (#230). Fn param flags (#233). Union props, const spreads, Pick/Record bases, etc. merged earlier.\n   - Ongoing: parity/oracle gates still fail on baseline drift; triage in PR #234 (draft); React-props policy in #235; collisions between open PRs; tap notifier PR unfinished.\n\n6. All user messages (this segment, user-role only, chronological; earliest context messages are in prior summary):\n   - \"But for things that storybook drops, consider that it might be doign so because prior it was too exepensive to do...\" (richer-by-default direction)\n   - \"Our agents are going to use monokl and monokl maybe can leverage calliper libraries??\"\n   - \"Monokl is being written already and it should not shell out. That is a cost we don't need to pay.\"\n   - \"For our primary use-case (storybook) let's work on continuing to improve accuracy and double-down on performance please. We're slipping on that end which is the whole point.\"\n   - \"Some PRs are not passing CI\"; \"Merged our PRs, what do we have outstanding?\"; \"The parity and gates seem to going for a long time no?\"; \"Accuracy taking over 34 min?\"; \"This is taking forever, I'm concerned\"; \"I don't trust you with the running task. It's taking too long\"; \"We need to finish the lazy work. It's holding up the rest\"\n   - \"Where are we in matching or exceeding RDT in quality and performance?\"; \"What is the x and % rate improvement estimated... per library...?\"; \"What's the average/median speed improvement and memory improvement?\"; \"And against normal react-docgen (ie storybook's built in)? Estimate is ok\"; \"That is great, accuracy of RDT at the speed of react-docgen. That is a huge gain.\"; \"Exact text is not important. Getting the types right is more important. And we actually get enums better than RDT in many places right?\"; \"All of this with opt-in obviously. I think the perf improvements even pan out there\"; \"I think the deps default should become on... And we're close to doing the same for on-deman TS 7.1 resolution\"\n   - \"Would be great to have some sort of 'score' for our confidence in the type detection...\"; \"We can use the oracle to estimate... Would BM25 or any other algorith help us in scoring this?\"; \"I would imagine speed is a bit more important than a small cost of memory at the edges right?...\"; \"Ok, let's find a good spot and let's see what we can do about not having a type prop gap using our ts 7.1 and any structural improvements\"\n   - \"Cleanup stale work and do an adversarial analysis on the codex work\"; \"Let's cleanup the codex worktree for any or merged already\"; \"We have two issues open that might be obsolete right?\"; \"What is the point of the LSP server?...\"; \"I'd say lets cleanup the LSP stuff then. Mostly duplicated code... Close the issue too\"\n   - \"Let's do everything you identified here please\"; \"And then start tackling what is left and the gaps\"; \"We should do the TS on demand. I though we had an add on no?\"; \"Let's commit, stack PRs appropriately. Remember to follow our rules for changesets. User focused, friendly, scannable right? You know what that means?\"\n   - \"FYI we have a homebrew tap repo now right?\"; \"What do we need to do here to allow auto-updating of the brew tap? We have a new action in our actions repo I think.\"; \"Are we staying consistent with addressing patterns, not special casing everything? Are there concerns we should address before we consider this PR done? Let's not be lazy\"\n   - \"We should not be ommiting @types/react props at all. Those are quite important ones, right? HOw can we fix that?\"; \"We should probably not exclude ref and we should dig into why we lost onClick, onFocus, etc. Those are crucial\"; \"FYI I setup the GH app, set the client ID and privte key for tap updating\"; \"Seems like we should go to full, it's faster and has a smaller JSON\"; \"Let's work on making it mergeable\"\n   - Also earlier this segment: \"Codex did a lot of the work while you were blocked. Can you check on it?\"; \"Is there a better way to do resolve? Are we doing this in native rust or using the TS 7.1 addon?\"; \"If we provide caliper as an NPM package for the base case it can depend on having ts 7.1 installed\"; \"Let's think and analyze deeply on strategies... bloom filters? round-robin? batch?\"; \"byt-scan prefilter might help for the extreme cases.\"; \"We should explore the ideas... without over-engineering\"; \"Are you sure about the byte-scan? ... older machine? a VM in GH actions?\"; \"Let's try it\"; \"We shouldn't merge and keep the temp workflow do we?\"; \"Speed and accuracy is the product\"; \"Don't forget that a 10,000 line redesign with agents might be at most a few hours.\"; \"We should definitely consider CI caches... But we do want that first start to also be mind blowingly fast\".\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - Finish the Homebrew notifier PR in caliper: commit `release.yml` change on `ci/notify-homebrew-tap`, push, open PR with base `main` (CI-only; no changeset), body ending with session URL; note requirements (actions#2 merged; org secret/var set), and that a real CLI release must exist for the tap to verify (0.6.0 incomplete per #222; #217 release PR open).\n   - \"Making it mergeable\": reconcile collisions among open PRs: ADR 0022 (#232 vs #233), cache schema 25 (#229 vs #233), allow-list entries (#229: 22, #230: 241, #233: 84, #234: 1) vs #224's check on main push (merge in bottom-up order, entries dropped after merge or fork-based cleanup), watch each PR's CI; get #235 (React props) and others verified.\n   - Running forks: `react-props` (told to flip default `htmlAttributes` to `full`, new ADR at next free number, re-measure vs old default on five corpora, run skipped gates, regenerate snapshots, friendly changeset) — PR #235 base `feat/semantic-on-demand`; `parity-triage` (PR #234 draft on `ci/repair-gates`: MenuTrigger fix `68c62e1f` done; classify remaining Parity rows, name second lost fixture component, tighten loose rest pattern, refresh baselines only for improvement/acceptable rows; mark ready only if Parity workflow passes).\n   - Open decisions for user: `key` stays omitted (ref included); order of merges; whether to merge #224 first; remote branch cleanup commands (`git push origin --delete scratch/parity-allow-probe` and earlier list); Codex's worktrees mostly removed; `agent-a482304b1a114609f` (old patch backed up