{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-10-26T21:26:19.200Z","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a72d9bb997d121f74</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01HVJ6nf3jAY7EK8vrUYwGTb</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-caliper--claude-worktrees-release-0-2-0/ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675/tasks/a72d9bb997d121f74.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Calibrate speed gate noise\" is waiting on its own background work</summary>\n<note>This agent stopped with background work of its own still running. It may resume on its own when that work completes or reports, and the same task-id notifies again if it does; the result below may be interim.</note>\n<result>Base-against-base noise is low enough that a wall-time gate at 1.10× (median of 9 runs per side) would have produced no false failures on CI. Peak RSS is noisier on CI, so I'd gate it at 1.20×, not 1.15×.\n\n**Task:** measure the A/A noise of the proposed speed and RSS gate. Method: `origin/main` at `668a7221`, release build, two copies of one binary, runs interleaved with alternating order, 15 runs per side per trial, 20 trials per corpus, `--json --no-cache`. Both local and CI ran 100 trials in total (20 per corpus across five corpora), and I ran two CI samples.\n\n**Wall time, head/base ratio (min / median / p95 / max):**\n\n| Corpus | Local, deps on | Local, deps off | CI run 1, deps off | CI run 2, deps off |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| mantine | 0.958 / 1.000 / 1.010 / 1.012 | 0.981 / 1.001 / 1.019 / 1.024 | 0.994 / 1.003 / 1.015 / 1.017 | 0.985 / 1.004 / 1.015 / 1.017 |\n| chakra | 0.978 / 0.993 / 1.021 / 1.043 | 0.975 / 1.000 / 1.012 / 1.013 | 0.968 / 0.995 / 1.015 / 1.020 | 0.973 / 1.005 / 1.017 / 1.017 |\n| shadcn | 0.896 / 0.999 / 1.028 / **1.220** | 0.979 / 0.998 / 1.025 / 1.030 | 0.977 / 1.000 / 1.017 / 1.022 | 0.976 / 1.006 / 1.027 / 1.033 |\n| antd | 0.910 / 0.988 / 1.012 / 1.027 | 0.982 / 1.009 / 1.037 / 1.052 | 0.980 / 1.005 / 1.021 / 1.035 | 0.975 / 0.995 / 1.008 / 1.010 |\n| spectrum | 0.939 / 1.003 / 1.035 / 1.056 | 0.979 / 0.998 / 1.024 / 1.031 | 0.972 / 1.004 / 1.022 / 1.024 | 0.977 / 0.995 / 1.015 / 1.017 |\n\n**Peak RSS, head/base ratio (p95 / max):**\n\n| Corpus | Local, deps on | CI run 1 | CI run 2 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| mantine | 1.010 / 1.011 | 1.048 / 1.068 | 1.046 / 1.047 |\n| chakra | 1.005 / 1.008 | 1.082 / 1.106 | 1.064 / 1.083 |\n| shadcn | 1.017 / 1.018 | 1.098 / 1.123 | 1.060 / 1.080 |\n| antd | 1.014 / 1.015 | 1.046 / 1.047 | 1.048 / 1.049 |\n| spectrum | 1.013 / 1.015 | 1.055 / 1.057 | 1.036 / 1.055 |\n\n**False-positive rate** (share of the 20 trials per corpus that exceed the threshold):\n\n| Check | 1.05 | 1.10 | 1.15 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Wall, CI (both runs, 200 trials) | 0% | 0% | 0% |\n| Wall, local deps on | shadcn 5%, spectrum 5% | shadcn 5% | shadcn 5% |\n| Wall, local deps off | antd 5% | 0% | 0% |\n| RSS, CI | 0–35% | 0–5% (chakra 0–5%, shadcn 0–5%) | 0% |\n| RSS, local | 0% | 0% | 0% |\n\n- **Re-run-once rule:** with a re-run, every wall and RSS false positive at 1.10 disappears, including local shadcn's one 1.22 outlier. That outlier happened while I was pushing and downloading in parallel, so the local deps-on figures are slightly pessimistic.\n- **Fewer runs:** subsampling the 15 runs, N=9 is as good as 15 on CI, with 0% false positives at 1.05 and 1.10 on every corpus. On CI, N=5 only slipped on chakra (5% at 1.05). Locally, antd needs N≥9. N=3 is too noisy (antd 20% at 1.05).\n\n**Runner differences:**\n- **CI:** AMD EPYC 7763 and AMD EPYC 9V74 (4 vCPU, 16 GB). Per-run wall time is 86–179 ms with dependency loading off. Wall noise is as small as locally.\n- **Local:** Apple machine (`Valinor-2`). I couldn't read the CPU model because `sysctl` is blocked in the sandbox. Per-run wall time is 38–241 ms.\n- **RSS:** much noisier on CI, with run-to-run swings up to 12%, against 2% locally. I haven't tested the cause. My guess is allocator and thread timing, so a single-thread RSS measurement might be quieter.\n\n**Scope notes:**\n- **Configuration:** the local runs used the installed corpora. The CI runs used the scorecard's sparse source-only corpora with `--no-load-dependencies`, which is the configuration the proposal's gate would use.\n- **Heavy path:** I didn't run CI with dependencies loaded. That would need the multi-GB parity installs.\n\n**Recommended gate:**\n- Wall time at **1.10×** (the 1.05 line sits inside observed noise locally).\n- RSS at **1.20×**, since 1.15 left only 0.03 of headroom over the worst CI sample.\n- Median of **9** interleaved runs per side, alternating which side goes first.\n- Re-run the failing corpus once before failing the job.\n- This also cuts the timed runs by about 40% against 15.\n\nA real regression is well clear of the gate: the ones we merged unseen were +38% and +47%. A gate this tight would also catch a +10% regression like `full`'s.\n\n**Scratch artifacts and cleanup:**\n- **Scratch branch:** `scratch/gate-calibration` (commits `9fc15694`, `d121d902`) holds `.github/workflows/gate-calibration.yml` and `scripts/aa.py`. It has no PR and I merged nothing.\n- **Delete the remote branch:** `git push origin --delete scratch/gate-calibration`\n- **Local worktree:** it's nested under this worktree at `.claude/worktrees/release-0.2.0/.claude/worktrees/gate-calibration`. Remove it with `git worktree remove` and delete the local branch.\n- **Result files:** in `$CLAUDE_JOB_DIR/tmp`: `aa-local.json`, `aa-local-nodeps.json`, `ci1/aa-ci.json`, `ci2/aa-ci.json`, plus `aa.py` and `aa_report.py`.\n\nresult: Base-against-base noise on the speed gate is low. Wall-time ratios stay within 1.037 at p95 on all five corpora on both CI runs and locally, so a 1.10× threshold gave no false failures on CI. Peak RSS is noisier on CI (up to 1.12×), so gate it at 1.20×. N=9 interleaved runs per side is enough, with a re-run-once rule as insurance.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>206361</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>50</tool_uses><duration_ms>1762974</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
