{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-10-27T00:53:22.684Z","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a9d2f6f8f9e04a5de</task-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-caliper--claude-worktrees-release-0-2-0/ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675/tasks/a9d2f6f8f9e04a5de.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Fix stack conflicts permanently\" is waiting on its own background work</summary>\n<note>This agent stopped with background work of its own still running. It may resume on its own when that work completes or reports, and the same task-id notifies again if it does; the result below may be interim.</note>\n<result>The open PRs now merge in sequence with no conflicts (simulated), and none has a failing check. `Entire Gates` was still evaluating on #229–#236 when I stopped, so those rows are not yet fully green. #243 holds the allow-list change, and I created no local branches.\n\n**What broke and where it started**\n- **Test merge damage** started at #229, when I merged #228's test into it. Git interleaved the two test functions, and the break spread up through #233, #235, #241 and #244. I rebuilt the test file with both tests whole, then merged that fix up the chain.\n- **Allow-list files:** git's rename detection copied #233's seven allow-list entries over #229's 51 in all four descendant branches. I restored #229's entries and checked every file's entry count.\n- **Entire Gates failures:** the gate wants each branch up to date with `main`. #228 merged, which put every branch 4 commits behind, so I merged `main` into all of them. This will recur after each merge you make.\n- **Flaky test:** #229's Rust test failed on a `tsc-client` timing test. I re-ran it and it passed.\n- **#244 Equivalence:** it needed its own allow-list file for one intended difference, Chakra `Group` now detected by `forwardRef`.\n\n**What changed**\n- **#243** (new, base `main`) replaces the shared allow-list file with `scripts/equivalence-allow.d/`, one file per pull request. Merge it first.\n- Each PR that carries entries now has its own file in that directory: #229 (51), #230 (243), #233 (7), #234 (1), #235 (112), #244 (1). The shared file is gone from all of them.\n\n| Order | PR | Base | Simulated merge | CI |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | #243 allow-list directory | main | ok | green |\n| 2 | #234 parity triage | main | ok | green; gate pending |\n| 3 | #230 | main | ok | green; gate pending |\n| 4 | #231 | main | ok | green; gate pending |\n| 5 | #236 Homebrew tap | main | ok | green; gate pending |\n| 6 | #229 | main | ok | green; gate pending |\n| 7 | #232 | main | ok | green; gate pending |\n| 8 | #233 | #229's branch | ok | green; gate pending |\n| 9 | #235 | #232's branch | ok | green; gate pending |\n| 10 | #241 | #235's branch | ok | green |\n| 11 | #244 | #241's branch | ok | green |\n| 12 | #240 docs | main | ok | green |\n\n#239 is the release PR and I left it alone.\n\n**Things to know**\n- **#229's base:** I could not retarget #229 onto #228, because GitHub locks the base of a PR that has a child PR (#233). It didn't matter, since #228 has already merged.\n- **README rows:** #233, #235 and their children carry merge commits of earlier PRs so the ADR README rows (0022, 0023, 0024) don't conflict. Their diffs vs. their base look larger until their parents merge.\n- **Stacked PR runs:** the Equivalence runs for #233, #235 and #241 compared against `main`, not their parent, so each one's allow-list directory must cover the whole stack. It now does.\n- **Compile check:** the combined result of all the PRs compiles and passes `cargo fmt --check`. Clippy and the extractor tests passed on #229's repaired head, and clippy on the top of the chain.\n- **Allow-list cleanup:** `main` must have no allow-list files, so each of the files above needs deleting after its PR merges. That is a single follow-up PR.\n- **BLOCKED:** #243 shows `BLOCKED` with every check passing, so that is probably a required review.\n- **Scratch files:** the scripts I used are in `$CLAUDE_JOB_DIR/tmp` (`sim_merge.py` for the simulation). I removed my scratch worktrees.\n\nresult: The open PRs (#229–#236, #240, #241, #243, #244) merge in sequence with no conflicts (simulated) and have no failing checks. The #229-origin test break is fixed and each PR has its own allow-list file in `scripts/equivalence-allow.d/` (#243); `Entire Gates` was still evaluating on #229–#236.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>314831</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>194</tool_uses><duration_ms>4049886</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
