{"parentUuid":"73e5b20a-f90b-4d50-9335-8e150d654f93","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgRpz3Y3WdTwyc4RGtgv9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The stack is ready to merge in sequence. The simulated merge passed for every open PR and none has a failing check, but `Entire Gates` was still evaluating on #229–#236 when the fork stopped. It reports 12 PRs; I confirmed only the earlier CI state, not this table.\n\n**What broke:**\n- **Test merge damage:** it started at #229, when #228's test was merged in. Git interleaved the two test functions, and the break spread up through #233, #235, #241 and #244. The fork rebuilt the file with both tests whole and merged the fix up the chain.\n- **Allow-list files:** git's rename detection copied #233's seven allow-list entries over #229's 51 in four descendant branches. The fork restored #229's entries and checked every file's count.\n- **Entire Gates:** it wants each branch up to date with `main`. #228 merged, which put every branch 4 commits behind, so the fork merged `main` into all of them. This will recur after each merge you make.\n- **Flaky test:** #229's Rust test failed on a `tsc-client` timing test and passed on re-run.\n\n**Merge order:**\n\n| Order | PR | Base |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | #243 allow-list directory | main |\n| 2 | #234 parity triage | main |\n| 3 | #230 | main |\n| 4 | #231 | main |\n| 5 | #236 Homebrew tap | main |\n| 6 | #229 | main |\n| 7 | #232 | main |\n| 8 | #233 | #229's branch |\n| 9 | #235 | #232's branch |\n| 10 | #241 | #235's branch |\n| 11 | #244 | #241's branch |\n| 12 | #240 docs | main |\n\n#239, the release PR, is untouched.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- **Merge #243 first.** It replaces the shared allow-list file with `scripts/equivalence-allow.d/`, one file per PR. Each PR carrying entries now has its own file: #229 (51), #230 (243), #233 (7), #234 (1), #235 (112), #244 (1).\n- **Allow-list cleanup:** `main` must have no allow-list files. After the merges, one follow-up PR deletes them.\n- **Branches behind `main`:** after each merge, `Entire Gates` may ask the next PR to be updated with `main`. Expect that and ask me to update the branches, or merge `main` in.\n- **#243 shows BLOCKED** with every check passing. That is probably a required review.\n- **Stacked Equivalence:** the Equivalence runs for #233, #235 and #241 compare against `main`, not their parent. Each allow-list directory covers the whole stack.\n- **README rows:** #233, #235 and their children carry earlier PRs' merge commits so the ADR README rows (0022–0024) don't conflict. Their diffs against their base look larger until their parents merge.\n- **#229's base:** it can't be retargeted onto #228, because GitHub locks the base of a PR that has a child. That no longer matters, since #228 merged.\n\nThe combined result of all the PRs compiles and passes `cargo fmt --check`. Clippy and the extractor tests passed on #229's repaired head, and clippy on the top of the chain. After the merges, the held-back follow-ups can start: the speed and output gates, the `apps/validate` deletion, and the ADR retirements.\n\nresult: The open PRs (#229–#236, #240, #241, #243, #244) merge in sequence with no conflicts in simulation and have no failing checks; `Entire Gates` was still evaluating on #229–#236. The #229-origin test break is fixed, and each PR has its own allow-list file under `scripts/equivalence-allow.d/` (#243, merge first)."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1915,"cache_read_input_tokens":185454,"output_tokens":1322,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":86},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1915,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":1322,"cac