{"parentUuid":"060c54a6-5340-4872-837e-4749719fef76","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgS8e8XDd49V6xV56RQ7X","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I can't reproduce conflicts. GitHub reports all 13 open PRs as MERGEABLE, and my simulation merges them in order cleanly against the current `main`, including the newly merged #245 and #228. The simulation covers #243, #234, #230, #231, #236, #229, #232, #233, #235, #241 and #240, in that order. All PRs still show BLOCKED, which isn't a conflict.\n\n**What I think you're hitting:** the \"branch must be up to date with `main`\" rule. Every time something merges, `main` gets a new merge commit that the other branches don't contain, so each one drops to \"behind\" and needs an update. That happens even when everything is stacked correctly, and with merge commits it never settles. It matches what the stacking fork found with Entire Gates, and it is why we keep updating branches by hand.\n\n**Options:**\n1. **Turn off \"require branches to be up to date\"** in branch protection and the Entire Gates rule. CI would still run on each PR, and the simulation covers conflicts. This is a repository setting, so it's your call.\n2. **Use a merge queue.** GitHub updates and tests each PR for you. It needs a one-time setup and is the proper fix if you want the up-to-date rule.\n3. **Make it one linear chain.** The `main`-based PRs would be rebased into a single sequence. That doesn't remove the up-to-date problem, and it adds new conflicts, so I don't recommend it.\n\nIf you're seeing conflicts on GitHub or locally on a particular PR, tell me which one and where, and I'll fix that branch right away. I'm not confident I'm looking at what you're looking at."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1019,"cache_read_input_tokens":190874,"output_tokens":1160,"output_tokens_