{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-10-27T04:59:13.026Z","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>aac9a646fcd533a07</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_019ZEKka2NYyDoUdgYrYsAfX</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-caliper--claude-worktrees-release-0-2-0/ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675/tasks/aac9a646fcd533a07.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Fix equivalence crash and scorecard flake\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I changed nothing, because neither failure is a bug in #230 or #231. `main` was force-pushed backwards, so the base each PR is compared against is now an old build.\n\n**Cause:** at 03:14:08Z, `castrog` force-pushed `main` from `ab051882` (the tip after #228 merged) back to `ef9136ed` (the #125 version-packages merge from Oct 23). The merge queue then merged #245 on top at 04:39Z. I confirmed this from the branch activity log, and `git merge-base --is-ancestor` shows that neither #224's merge commit (`d17de083`) nor #228's (`ab051882`) is reachable from `origin/main`. `main` has 410 files, against 482 on #230's head.\n\n**Lost from `main`:** #203–#228 and the other Oct 26 merges. That includes `scripts/equivalence.py`, which `main` no longer has. The old tip `ab051882` is still on GitHub and recoverable. The PR branches contain that history, but the Oct 26 merges are not on `main` until those branches merge.\n\n**#230 Equivalence crash (`KeyError: 'WebkitAppearance'`):**\n- The job runs the PR's `scripts/equivalence.py`, which expects the newer interned prop-set and name-list output, against a base binary built from `main`.\n- That binary is now the Oct 23 build, and its output doesn't match the script's format, so the lookup fails.\n- I did not reproduce the crash locally or confirm the output formats, so this is the likely cause, not a verified one.\n- #230 itself is probably fine, so there is no regression test to add and no script fix to stack on #243.\n\n**#231 Scorecard (chakra 103.4 ms vs main's 71.7 ms):** the \"main\" side is the Oct 23 build, which is faster than the Oct 26 work. So the 1.44× is not noise or a regression in #231, which touches only storybook export code. I re-ran the job once (`gh run rerun 40451135263 --failed`), and it will compare against the same old base, so I wouldn't trust its result either way.\n\n**Also noticed:** the merge queue is active. `main` merges go through `github-merge-queue[bot]` (the bot appears throughout the activity log), which explains the `gh-readonly-queue` branches in the event feed.\n\n**Decision for you:**\n- If the rewind was deliberate, for example to remove something from history, then every open PR's CI will keep comparing against the wrong base. Say so and I'll work out the best merge order from the old state.\n- If it was accidental, restoring `main` means resetting it to `ab051882`, followed by `#245`'s one commit. That is a force-push, so it has to be you.\n\nI pushed nothing and made no commits. The fork `sync-main` already merged the post-rewind `main` into the PR branches, so they hold two extra commits from #245. That is harmless, but note it if you restore `main`.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>222040</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>17</tool_uses><duration_ms>75728</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
