sn't match the script's format, so the lookup fails.\n- I did not reproduce the crash locally or confirm the output formats, so this is the likely cause, not a verified one.\n- #230 itself is probably fine, so there is no regression test to add and no script fix to stack on #243.\n\n**#231 Scorecard (chakra 103.4 ms vs main's 71.7 ms):** the \"main\" side is the Oct 23 build, which is faster than the Oct 26 work. So the 1.44× is not noise or a regression in #231, which touches only storybook export code. I re-ran the job once (`gh run rerun 40451135263 --failed`), and it will compare against the same old base, so I wouldn't trust its result either way.\n\n**Also noticed:** the merge queue is active. `main` merges go through `github-merge-queue[bot]` (the bot appears throughout the activity log), which explains the `gh-readonly-queue` branches in the event feed.\n\n**Decision for you:**\n- If the rewind was deliberate, for example to remove something from history, then every open PR's CI will keep comparing against the wrong base. Say so and I'll work out the best merge order from the old state.\n- If it was accidental, restoring `main` means resetting it to `ab051882`, followed by `#245`'s one commit. That is a force-push, so it has to be you.\n\nI pushed nothing and made no commits. The fork `sync-main` already merged the post-rewind `main` into the PR branches, so they hold two extra commits from #245. That is harmless, but note it if you restore `main`.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>222040</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>17</tool_uses><duration_ms>75728</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"},"uuid":"4d18b7ed-de86-4127-92f1-547e46762f74","timestamp":"2026-10-27T04:59:13.038Z","permissionMode":"auto","origin":{"kind":"task-notification","producer":"session-task","runId":"0mvq7sliz-026ee0cb"},"promptSource":"system","turnOrigin":"task_notification","turnPosition":{"promptIndex":222,"turnIndex":352},"queueSkipAttachments":true,"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"ex