{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-10-27T04:59:16.812Z","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a9d2f6f8f9e04a5de</task-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-caliper--claude-worktrees-release-0-2-0/ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675/tasks/a9d2f6f8f9e04a5de.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Fix stack conflicts permanently\" is waiting on its own background work</summary>\n<note>This agent stopped with background work of its own still running. It may resume on its own when that work completes or reports, and the same task-id notifies again if it does; the result below may be interim.</note>\n<result>needs input: `main` was force-pushed back to an old commit, so everything in my stack and CI is now being measured against the wrong base. I stopped pushing and did not touch `main`. I'm not a fork the user can ask directly, so please relay this.\n\n**What happened.** GitHub's activity log for `main`:\n\n| Time (UTC) | Event | Before → after |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2026-10-26 23:57 | merge queue merges #228 | `668a7221` → `ab051882` |\n| 2026-10-27 03:14 | **`castrog` force-push** | `ab051882` → `ef9136ed` (the old #125 version-packages commit) |\n| 2026-10-27 04:39 | merge queue merges #245 (fix-entire) | `ef9136ed` → `b7fb9a2b` |\n\n`main` is now 295 commits behind `ab051882`. It has lost #127 through #228, including lazy resolution, the equivalence and parity scripts, the workflows and the ADRs.\n\n**Why CI is red.** Every open PR now merges and diffs against a `main` that lacks the work its branch builds on.\n- **#230 and #231:** Equivalence crashes with `KeyError: 'WebkitAppearance'`.\n- **#231:** the Scorecard fails with chakra at 1.44× versus main.\n- **The rest of the open PRs:** they all restarted CI when someone pushed fresh merges into them. Those runs will meet the same problem.\n\nI did not investigate these failures further, because they are measured against the wrong base.\n\n**Fix, for you to run.** Restore `main` to the last good commit and re-apply #245 on top of it:\n1. Reset `main` to `ab051882`: `git push --force-with-lease=main:b7fb9a2bce61f3346a163b9ebf011b84bcf6ed36 origin ab051882:main`. This needs your admin rights, because branch protection and the merge queue may block a force-push.\n2. Re-merge #245's change (`0f1b84df`, \"chore: fix entire and cleanup hooks\") on a branch off `ab051882` and open a PR. It deleted the `.cursor` and `.opencode` hook files, so check it against the restored tree.\n\nAfter that I rerun the simulation, merge the restored `main` into each branch and watch CI to green. I can start immediately once `main` is fixed.\n\n**State of the stack before the reset.** `scripts/sim_merge.py` reported all of #243, #234, #230, #231, #236, #229, #232, #233, #235, #241, #244 and #240 as merging cleanly in that order. The final run of that simulation was against the broken `main`, so it proves nothing now. CI had no failures on the earlier heads, and #241, #243 and #244 had all checks passing.\n\n**What I changed in this task (all pushed without force):**\n- **#243** replaces the shared allow-list file with `scripts/equivalence-allow.d/`, one file per PR.\n- Each PR that lists differences now has its own file there: #229 (51), #230 (243), #233 (7), #234 (1), #235 (112), #244 (1).\n- I repaired the test merge in #229 and propagated the fix up #233, #235, #241 and #244. Compile, fmt and clippy passed on the repaired heads.\n- I restored #229's allow-list entries after git carried #233's over them, and merged main into every branch for the Entire \"up to date\" gate.\n- I removed my scratch worktrees.\n\nThe sandbox wouldn't let me edit files under the jobs directory with Bash, so the scratch scripts there are written with the editor tools only.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>339644</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>215</tool_uses><duration_ms>18789772</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
