 yes |\n| #235 | `f4f3b33b` | `4a8eb9c6` | yes |\n| #241 | `93e4b8c5` | `531df188` | yes |\n| #244 | `1ee42d60` | `feae41bf` | yes |\n\n- **Method:** my first attempt squashed each branch to one commit. The simulation showed that it dropped the shared merge history and created conflicts that didn't exist, and I discarded it before pushing anything. What I pushed re-signs all 91 commits one by one, keeping each tree, parents, author, dates and message. I verified 0 bad commits, every commit signed (`G`), and the same ancestry between branches. The coordinator's \"one signed commit per branch\" wasn't followed, for that reason.\n- **Merged afterwards:** #229, #233 and #240 merged into `main` through the re-signed commits, so those commits are now in `main`'s history.\n- **Not touched:** #243, #234, #236, #230 and #231 had merged before I pushed anything. Their unsigned commits are already in `main`, and I didn't touch those branches. #239 and #248 are unchanged.\n- **Backup:** old heads are in `$CLAUDE_JOB_DIR/tmp/old-heads.txt`.\n\n**Follow-up pushes** (normal fast-forwards, signed merge commits) after the three merges moved `main`:\n\n| PR | Base | Head | State |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| #232 | main | `717425bc` | mergeable; GitHub's earlier \"conflicting\" cleared; 15 checks pending |\n| #235 | #232's branch | `5915bc5e` | mergeable; 16 pending |\n| #241 | #235's branch | `5f533472` | mergeable; 17 pending |\n| #244 | #241's branch | `0c82e01e` | mergeable; 17 pending |\n\n**Signing rule:** the ruleset on `main` includes `required_signatures`, `merge_queue` and `non_fast_forward`, so signed commits do matter here. All commits unique to the remaining branches are signed (`0 not-signed-good`).\n\n**New PR #252** empties `scripts/equivalence-allow.d/`. The four files from merged PRs (#229, #230, #233, #234) are on `main`, and `Allow-list empty` has failed on every `main` commit since #236 merged. Merge it next, before the rest of the chain.\n\n**#239 (release PR):** left